window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-12-2", "source": "2021年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay related to the short passage given below. In your essay, you are to comment on the phenomenon described in the passage and suggest measures to address the issue. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.\n\nNowadays star chasing is prevalent among many teenagers. They take pop stars as their idols, imitating their way of talking, following their style of dressing9 and seeking every chance to meet them in person at great expenses.", "sample": "The passage vividly presents a common scene: many teenagers today are obsessed with pop stars, copying how\ntheir idols talk and dress and spending heavily just to see them face to face. In my view, this phenomenon\ndeserves a balanced judgement rather than blind praise or total denial.\nAdmittedly, taking an admirable star as one's idol can be positively motivating, since a hard-working singer\nmay inspire young people to pursue their own dreams. However, when admiration turns into blind worship, the\nharm outweighs the benefit. Chasing stars at great expense drains teenagers' limited money and time, distracts\nthem from their studies, and may even distort their values.\nTo address the issue, joint efforts are needed. Parents and schools should guide adolescents to build a rational\nview of idols, while the media ought to promote genuine role models instead of empty fame. For teenagers\nthemselves, the key is to learn from a star's strengths without losing themselves. Only in this way can idol\nworship become a source of growth rather than regret."}, "translation": {"prompt": "延安位于陕西省北部，地处黄河中游，是中国革命的圣地。毛泽东等老一辈革命家曾在这里生活战斗了十三个春秋，领导了抗日战争和解放战争，培育了延安精神，为中国革命做出了巨大贡献。延安的革命旧址全国数量最大、分布最广，级别最高。延安是全国爱国主义、革命传统和延安精神教育基地。延安有9个革命纪念馆，珍藏着中共中央和老一辈革命家在延安时期留存下来的大量重要物品，因此享有 \"中国革命博物馆城\" 的美誉。", "sample": "Located in northern Shaanxi Province on the middle reaches of the Yellow River, Yan'an is the holy land of the Chinese revolution. Mao Zedong and other revolutionaries of the older generation once lived and fought here for thirteen years, led the War of Resistance Against Japanese Aggression and the War of Liberation, fostered the Yan'an Spirit, and made great contributions to the Chinese revolution. Yan'an's revolutionary sites are the largest in number, the most widely distributed and the highest in rank in the whole country. Moreover, it is a national base for education in patriotism, revolutionary traditions and the Yan'an Spirit. Yan'an has nine revolutionary memorial halls, which house a large number of important items left by the CPC Central Committee and older-generation revolutionaries during the Yan'an period, and thus enjoys the reputation of \"the City of Museums of the Chinese Revolution\"."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2021-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "According to psychologist Sharon Draper, our clothing choices can absolutely affect our well-being. When we wear ill-fitting clothes, or feel over- or under-dressed for an event, it's natural to feel self-conscious or even stressed. Conversely, she says, opting for clothes that fit well and  __26__  with your sense of style can improve your confidence.\nBut can you improve your health through your  __27__  clothing, without having to dash out and buy a whole new  __28__  ? \"Absolutely,\" says Draper. If your goal is to improve your thinking, she recommends picking clothes that fit well and are unlikely to encourage restlessness, so avoid bows, ties and unnecessary  __29__  . It also helps to opt for clothes you  __30__  as tying in with your goals, so if you want to perform better at work, select pieces you view as professional. Draper says this fits in with the concept of behavioral activation, whereby  __31__  in a behavior (in this case, selecting clothes) can set you on the path to then achieving your goals (working harder).\nAnother way to improve your  __32__  of mind is to mix things up. Draper says we often feel stuck in a rut(常规) if we wear the same clothes—even if they're our favorites—thus opting for an item you don't wear often, or adding something different to an outfit, such as a hat, can  __33__  shift your mood. On days when you're really  __34__  to brave the world, Draper suggests selecting sentimental items of clothing, such as ones you wore on a special day, or given to you by a loved one, as clothes with  __35__  associations can help you tap into constructive emotions.", "matchIntro": "Do music lessons really make children smarter?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "A recent analysis found that most research mischaracterizes the relationship between music and skills enhancement."}, {"label": "B", "text": "In 2004, a paper appeared in the journal Psychological Science, titled \"Music Lessons Enhance IQ.\" The author, composer and psychologist Glenn Schellenberg had conducted an experiment with 144 children randomly assigned to four groups: one learned the keyboard for a year, one took singing lessons, one joined an acting class, and a control group had no extracurricular training. The IQ of the children in the two musical groups rose by an average of seven points in the course of a year; those in the other two groups gained an average of 4.3 points."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Schellenberg had long been skeptical of the science supporting claims that music education enhances children's abstract reasoning, math, or language skills. If children who play the piano are smarter, he says, it doesn't necessarily mean they are smarter because they play the piano. It could be that the youngsters who play the piano also happen to be more ambitious or better at focusing on a task. Correlation, after all, does not prove causation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The 2004 paper was specifically designed to address those concerns. And as a passionate musician, Schellenberg was delighted when he turned up credible evidence that music has transfer effects on general intelligence. But nearly a decade later, in 2013, the Education Endowment Foundation funded a bigger study with more than 900 students. That study failed to confirm Schellenberg's findings, producing no evidence that music lessons improved math and literacy skills."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Schellenberg took that news in stride while continuing to cast a skeptical eye on the research in his field. Recently, he decided to formally investigate just how often his fellow researchers in psychology and neuroscience make what he believes are erroneous—or at least premature—causal connections between music and intelligence. His results, published in May, suggest that many of his peers do just that."}, {"label": "F", "text": "For his recent study, Schellenberg asked two research assistants to look for correlational studies on the effects of music education. They found a total of 114 papers published since 2000. To assess whether the authors claimed any causation, researchers then looked for telltale verbs in each paper's title and abstract, verbs like \"enhance,\" \"promote,\" \"facilitate,\" and \"strengthen.\" The papers were categorized as neuroscience if the study employed a brain imaging method like magnetic resonance, or if the study appeared in a journal that had \"brain,\" \"neuroscience,\" or a related term in its title. Otherwise the papers were categorized as psychology. Schellenberg didn't tell his assistants what exactly he was trying to prove."}, {"label": "G", "text": "After computing their assessments, Schellenberg concluded that the majority of the articles erroneously claimed that music training had a causal effect. The overselling, he also found, was more prevalent among neuroscience studies, three quarters of which mischaracterized a mere association between music training and skills enhancement as a cause-and-effect relationship. This may come as a surprise to some. Psychologists have been battling charges that they don't do \"real\" science for some time—in large part because many findings from classic experiments have proved unreproducible. Neuroscientists, on the other hand, armed with brain scans and EEGs(脑电图), have not been subject to the same degree of critique."}, {"label": "H", "text": "To argue for a cause-and-effect relationship, scientists must attempt to explain why and how a connection could occur. When it comes to transfer effects of music, scientists frequently point to brain plasticity—the fact that the brain changes according to how we use it. When a child learns to play the violin, for example, several studies have shown that the brain region responsible for the fine motor skills of the left hand's fingers is likely to grow. And many experiments have shown that musical training improves certain hearing capabilities, like filtering voices from background noise or distinguishing the difference between the consonants(辅音) 'b' and 'g'."}, {"label": "I", "text": "But Schellenberg remains highly critical of how the concept of plasticity has been applied in his field. \"Plasticity has become an industry of its own,\" he wrote in his May paper. Practice does change the brain, he allows, but what is questionable is the assertion that these changes affect other brain regions, such as those responsible for spatial reasoning or math problems."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Neuropsychologist Lutz Jancke agrees. \"Most of these studies don't allow for causal inferences,\" he said. For over two decades, Jancke has researched the effects of music lessons, and like Schellenberg, he believes that the only way to truly understand their effects is to run longitudinal studies. In such studies, researchers would need to follow groups of children with and without music lessons over a long period of time—even if the assignments are not completely random. Then they could compare outcomes for each group."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Some researchers are starting to do just that. The neuroscientist Peter Schneider from Heidelberg University in Germany, for example, has been following a group of children for ten years now. Some of them were handed musical instruments and given lessons through a school-based program in the Ruhr region of Germany called Jedem Kind ein Instrument, or \"an instrument for every child,\" which was carried out with government funding. Among these children, Schneider has found that those who were enthusiastic about music and who practiced voluntarily showed improvements in hearing ability, as well as in more general competencies, such as the ability to concentrate."}, {"label": "L", "text": "To establish whether effects such as improved concentration are caused by music participation itself, and not by investing time in an extracurricular activity of any kind, Assal Habibi, a psychology professor at the University of Southern California, is conducting a five-year longitudinal study with children from low-income communities in Los Angeles. The youngsters fall into three groups: those who take after-school music, those who do after-school sports, and those with no structured after-school program at all. After two years, Habibi and her colleagues reported seeing structural changes in the brains of the musically trained children, both locally and in the pathways connecting different parts of the brain."}, {"label": "M", "text": "That may seem compelling, but Habibi's children were not selected randomly. Did the children who were drawn to music perhaps have something in them from the start that made them different but eluded the brain scanners? \"As somebody who started taking piano lessons at the age of five and got up every morning at seven to practice, that experience changed me and made me part of who I am today,\" Schellenberg said. \"The question is whether those kinds of experiences do so systematically across individuals and create exactly the same changes. And I think that is that huge leap of faith.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "Did he have a hidden talent that others didn't have? Or more endurance than his peers? Music researchers tend, like Schellenberg, to be musicians themselves, and as he noted in his recent paper, \"the idea of positive cognitive and neural side effects from music training (and other pleasurable activities) is inherently appealing.\" He also admits that if he had children of his own, he would encourage them to take music lessons and go to university. \"I would think that it makes them better people, more critical, just wiser in general,\" he said."}, {"label": "O", "text": "But those convictions should be checked at the entrance to the lab, he added. Otherwise, the work becomes religion or faith. \"You have to let go of your faith if you want to be a scientist.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The trend toward rationality and enlightenment was endangered long before the advent of the World Wide Web. As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television introduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment. In an image-centered and pleasure-driven world, Postman noted, there is no place for rational thinking, because you simply cannot think with images. It is text that enables us to \"uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect abuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions, to connect one generalization to another.\"\nThe dominance of television was not confined to our living rooms. It overturned all of those habits of mind, fundamentally changing our experience of the world, affecting the conduct of politics, religion, business, and culture. It reduced many aspects of modern life to entertainment, sensationalism, and commerce. \"Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other,\" Postman wrote. \"They don't exchange ideas, they exchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials.\"\nAt first, the web seemed to push against this trend. When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure. Reason and thought were most valued in this garden—all derived from the project of the Enlightenment. Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new medium, which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs, electronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums. It was an intellectual project, not about commerce or control, created in a scientific research center in Switzerland. And for more than a decade, the web created an alternative space that threatened television's grip on society.\nSocial networks, though, have since colonized the web for television's values. From Facebook to Instagram, the medium refocuses our attention on videos and images, rewarding emotional appeals—'like' buttons—over rational ones. Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing. (It's telling that, while Google began life as a PhD thesis, Facebook started as a tool to judge classmates, appearances.) It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and think, based on our profiles and preferences. The Enlightenment's motto(座右铭) of 'Dare to know' has become 'Dare not to care to know.'", "47": "The trend toward rationality and enlightenment was endangered long before the advent of the World Wide Web. As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television introduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment. In an image-centered and pleasure-driven world, Postman noted, there is no place for rational thinking, because you simply cannot think with images. It is text that enables us to \"uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect abuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions, to connect one generalization to another.\"\nThe dominance of television was not confined to our living rooms. It overturned all of those habits of mind, fundamentally changing our experience of the world, affecting the conduct of politics, religion, business, and culture. It reduced many aspects of modern life to entertainment, sensationalism, and commerce. \"Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other,\" Postman wrote. \"They don't exchange ideas, they exchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials.\"\nAt first, the web seemed to push against this trend. When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure. Reason and thought were most valued in this garden—all derived from the project of the Enlightenment. Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new medium, which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs, electronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums. It was an intellectual project, not about commerce or control, created in a scientific research center in Switzerland. And for more than a decade, the web created an alternative space that threatened television's grip on society.\nSocial networks, though, have since colonized the web for television's values. From Facebook to Instagram, the medium refocuses our attention on videos and images, rewarding emotional appeals—'like' buttons—over rational ones. Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing. (It's telling that, while Google began life as a PhD thesis, Facebook started as a tool to judge classmates, appearances.) It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and think, based on our profiles and preferences. The Enlightenment's motto(座右铭) of 'Dare to know' has become 'Dare not to care to know.'", "48": "The trend toward rationality and enlightenment was endangered long before the advent of the World Wide Web. As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television introduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment. In an image-centered and pleasure-driven world, Postman noted, there is no place for rational thinking, because you simply cannot think with images. It is text that enables us to \"uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect abuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions, to connect one generalization to another.\"\nThe dominance of television was not confined to our living rooms. It overturned all of those habits of mind, fundamentally changing our experience of the world, affecting the conduct of politics, religion, business, and culture. It reduced many aspects of modern life to entertainment, sensationalism, and commerce. \"Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other,\" Postman wrote. \"They don't exchange ideas, they exchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials.\"\nAt first, the web seemed to push against this trend. When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure. Reason and thought were most valued in this garden—all derived from the project of the Enlightenment. Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new medium, which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs, electronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums. It was an intellectual project, not about commerce or control, created in a scientific research center in Switzerland. And for more than a decade, the web created an alternative space that threatened television's grip on society.\nSocial networks, though, have since colonized the web for television's values. From Facebook to Instagram, the medium refocuses our attention on videos and images, rewarding emotional appeals—'like' buttons—over rational ones. Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing. (It's telling that, while Google began life as a PhD thesis, Facebook started as a tool to judge classmates, appearances.) It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and think, based on our profiles and preferences. The Enlightenment's motto(座右铭) of 'Dare to know' has become 'Dare not to care to know.'", "49": "The trend toward rationality and enlightenment was endangered long before the advent of the World Wide Web. As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television introduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment. In an image-centered and pleasure-driven world, Postman noted, there is no place for rational thinking, because you simply cannot think with images. It is text that enables us to \"uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect abuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions, to connect one generalization to another.\"\nThe dominance of television was not confined to our living rooms. It overturned all of those habits of mind, fundamentally changing our experience of the world, affecting the conduct of politics, religion, business, and culture. It reduced many aspects of modern life to entertainment, sensationalism, and commerce. \"Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other,\" Postman wrote. \"They don't exchange ideas, they exchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials.\"\nAt first, the web seemed to push against this trend. When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure. Reason and thought were most valued in this garden—all derived from the project of the Enlightenment. Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new medium, which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs, electronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums. It was an intellectual project, not about commerce or control, created in a scientific research center in Switzerland. And for more than a decade, the web created an alternative space that threatened television's grip on society.\nSocial networks, though, have since colonized the web for television's values. From Facebook to Instagram, the medium refocuses our attention on videos and images, rewarding emotional appeals—'like' buttons—over rational ones. Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing. (It's telling that, while Google began life as a PhD thesis, Facebook started as a tool to judge classmates, appearances.) It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and think, based on our profiles and preferences. The Enlightenment's motto(座右铭) of 'Dare to know' has become 'Dare not to care to know.'", "50": "The trend toward rationality and enlightenment was endangered long before the advent of the World Wide Web. As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television introduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment. In an image-centered and pleasure-driven world, Postman noted, there is no place for rational thinking, because you simply cannot think with images. It is text that enables us to \"uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect abuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions, to connect one generalization to another.\"\nThe dominance of television was not confined to our living rooms. It overturned all of those habits of mind, fundamentally changing our experience of the world, affecting the conduct of politics, religion, business, and culture. It reduced many aspects of modern life to entertainment, sensationalism, and commerce. \"Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other,\" Postman wrote. \"They don't exchange ideas, they exchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials.\"\nAt first, the web seemed to push against this trend. When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure. Reason and thought were most valued in this garden—all derived from the project of the Enlightenment. Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new medium, which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs, electronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums. It was an intellectual project, not about commerce or control, created in a scientific research center in Switzerland. And for more than a decade, the web created an alternative space that threatened television's grip on society.\nSocial networks, though, have since colonized the web for television's values. From Facebook to Instagram, the medium refocuses our attention on videos and images, rewarding emotional appeals—'like' buttons—over rational ones. Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing. (It's telling that, while Google began life as a PhD thesis, Facebook started as a tool to judge classmates, appearances.) It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and think, based on our profiles and preferences. The Enlightenment's motto(座右铭) of 'Dare to know' has become 'Dare not to care to know.'", "51": "According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to some degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take. It can lead to workplace dysfunction and diminish their own job satisfaction. I'm not referring to employees who are legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say, being denied fair pay or flexible work practices. I'm talking about those who consistently believe they deserve special treatment and generous rewards. It's an expectation that exists irrespective of their abilities or levels of performance.\nAs a result of that discrepancy between the privileges they feel they're owed and their inflated sense of self-worth, they don't work as hard for their employer. They prefer instead to slack off. It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who overindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment throughout their adult lives. And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to nonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened motivation, to perform well.\nThe research team from several American universities surveyed more than 240 individuals. They sampled managers as well as team members. Employee entitlement was measured by statements such as \"I honestly feel I'm just more deserving than others.\" The respondents had to rate the extent of their agreement. Employee engagement, meanwhile, was assessed with statements like \"I really throw myself into my work.\" The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of employee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them, ethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations. They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors and are genuinely committed to doing the right thing. Additionally, these leaders are consistent in their standards. They're also less likely to deviate in how they treat employees.\nThis means, when confronted by an entitled team member, an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands. He or she will instead point out, constructively and tactfully, exactly how their inflated sense of deservingness is somewhat distorted. They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the employee must meet to receive their desired rewards. This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel more confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're perceived to be fair and trustworthy.\nThe researchers, however, exercise caution by warning no one single response is the perfect remedy. But there's no denying ethical leadership is at least a critical step in the right direction.", "52": "According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to some degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take. It can lead to workplace dysfunction and diminish their own job satisfaction. I'm not referring to employees who are legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say, being denied fair pay or flexible work practices. I'm talking about those who consistently believe they deserve special treatment and generous rewards. It's an expectation that exists irrespective of their abilities or levels of performance.\nAs a result of that discrepancy between the privileges they feel they're owed and their inflated sense of self-worth, they don't work as hard for their employer. They prefer instead to slack off. It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who overindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment throughout their adult lives. And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to nonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened motivation, to perform well.\nThe research team from several American universities surveyed more than 240 individuals. They sampled managers as well as team members. Employee entitlement was measured by statements such as \"I honestly feel I'm just more deserving than others.\" The respondents had to rate the extent of their agreement. Employee engagement, meanwhile, was assessed with statements like \"I really throw myself into my work.\" The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of employee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them, ethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations. They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors and are genuinely committed to doing the right thing. Additionally, these leaders are consistent in their standards. They're also less likely to deviate in how they treat employees.\nThis means, when confronted by an entitled team member, an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands. He or she will instead point out, constructively and tactfully, exactly how their inflated sense of deservingness is somewhat distorted. They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the employee must meet to receive their desired rewards. This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel more confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're perceived to be fair and trustworthy.\nThe researchers, however, exercise caution by warning no one single response is the perfect remedy. But there's no denying ethical leadership is at least a critical step in the right direction.", "53": "According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to some degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take. It can lead to workplace dysfunction and diminish their own job satisfaction. I'm not referring to employees who are legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say, being denied fair pay or flexible work practices. I'm talking about those who consistently believe they deserve special treatment and generous rewards. It's an expectation that exists irrespective of their abilities or levels of performance.\nAs a result of that discrepancy between the privileges they feel they're owed and their inflated sense of self-worth, they don't work as hard for their employer. They prefer instead to slack off. It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who overindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment throughout their adult lives. And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to nonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened motivation, to perform well.\nThe research team from several American universities surveyed more than 240 individuals. They sampled managers as well as team members. Employee entitlement was measured by statements such as \"I honestly feel I'm just more deserving than others.\" The respondents had to rate the extent of their agreement. Employee engagement, meanwhile, was assessed with statements like \"I really throw myself into my work.\" The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of employee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them, ethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations. They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors and are genuinely committed to doing the right thing. Additionally, these leaders are consistent in their standards. They're also less likely to deviate in how they treat employees.\nThis means, when confronted by an entitled team member, an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands. He or she will instead point out, constructively and tactfully, exactly how their inflated sense of deservingness is somewhat distorted. They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the employee must meet to receive their desired rewards. This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel more confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're perceived to be fair and trustworthy.\nThe researchers, however, exercise caution by warning no one single response is the perfect remedy. But there's no denying ethical leadership is at least a critical step in the right direction.", "54": "According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to some degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take. It can lead to workplace dysfunction and diminish their own job satisfaction. I'm not referring to employees who are legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say, being denied fair pay or flexible work practices. I'm talking about those who consistently believe they deserve special treatment and generous rewards. It's an expectation that exists irrespective of their abilities or levels of performance.\nAs a result of that discrepancy between the privileges they feel they're owed and their inflated sense of self-worth, they don't work as hard for their employer. They prefer instead to slack off. It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who overindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment throughout their adult lives. And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to nonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened motivation, to perform well.\nThe research team from several American universities surveyed more than 240 individuals. They sampled managers as well as team members. Employee entitlement was measured by statements such as \"I honestly feel I'm just more deserving than others.\" The respondents had to rate the extent of their agreement. Employee engagement, meanwhile, was assessed with statements like \"I really throw myself into my work.\" The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of employee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them, ethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations. They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors and are genuinely committed to doing the right thing. Additionally, these leaders are consistent in their standards. They're also less likely to deviate in how they treat employees.\nThis means, when confronted by an entitled team member, an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands. He or she will instead point out, constructively and tactfully, exactly how their inflated sense of deservingness is somewhat distorted. They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the employee must meet to receive their desired rewards. This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel more confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're perceived to be fair and trustworthy.\nThe researchers, however, exercise caution by warning no one single response is the perfect remedy. But there's no denying ethical leadership is at least a critical step in the right direction.", "55": "According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to some degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take. It can lead to workplace dysfunction and diminish their own job satisfaction. I'm not referring to employees who are legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say, being denied fair pay or flexible work practices. I'm talking about those who consistently believe they deserve special treatment and generous rewards. It's an expectation that exists irrespective of their abilities or levels of performance.\nAs a result of that discrepancy between the privileges they feel they're owed and their inflated sense of self-worth, they don't work as hard for their employer. They prefer instead to slack off. It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who overindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment throughout their adult lives. And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to nonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened motivation, to perform well.\nThe research team from several American universities surveyed more than 240 individuals. They sampled managers as well as team members. Employee entitlement was measured by statements such as \"I honestly feel I'm just more deserving than others.\" The respondents had to rate the extent of their agreement. Employee engagement, meanwhile, was assessed with statements like \"I really throw myself into my work.\" The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of employee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them, ethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations. They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors and are genuinely committed to doing the right thing. Additionally, these leaders are consistent in their standards. They're also less likely to deviate in how they treat employees.\nThis means, when confronted by an entitled team member, an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands. He or she will instead point out, constructively and tactfully, exactly how their inflated sense of deservingness is somewhat distorted. They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the employee must meet to receive their desired rewards. This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel more confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're perceived to be fair and trustworthy.\nThe researchers, however, exercise caution by warning no one single response is the perfect remedy. But there's no denying ethical leadership is at least a critical step in the right direction."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman's new book?", "options": ["A) It has given rise to much controversy.", "B) It has been very favorably received.", "C) It was primarily written for vegetarians.", "D) It offends many environmentalists."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has given rise to much controversy.\n【定位】With me today is Meghan Brown, an environmental activist whose controversial new book\nBeyond Recycling is making headlines.\n今天和我在一起的是梅根·布朗，一位环保活动家，她那本充满争议的新书《超越回收》正成\n为头条。\n【信号】开场介绍即定性句。主持人开场介绍嘉宾时用 controversial new book 直接给书定了性，\n开场点题句往往就是答案句，不必等转折。\n【替换】controversial new book ↔ It has given rise to much controversy.\n形容词转名词化\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nIt has been very favorably received. 说广受好评，与原文 controversial(有争议)方向相\n反；有争议恰恰意味着评价两极，不等于一致叫好。\n借⽤原⽂：controversial new book\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt was primarily written for vegetarians. 借了后文反复出现的素食话题，但录音从未说这\n本书「主要写给素食者」；vegetarian 是真听到的词，可这句话原文没说，是典型听到原词\n就想选。\n借⽤原⽂：They know I'm a vegetarian\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt offends many environmentalists. 用了 environmentalist 这个反复出现的词，但原文\n说她被人「描绘成」环保活动家，从没说这本书「冒犯了许多环保人士」，属凭原词臆造。\n借⽤原⽂：as a radical animal rights activist and environmentalist", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do some critics say about the author of the book?", "options": ["A) She neglects people's efforts in animal protection.", "B) She tries to force people to accept her radical ideas.", "C) She ignores the various benefits of public transport.", "D) She insists vegetarians are harming the environment."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） She tries to force people to accept her radical ideas.\n【定位】Critics of your book assert that you're trying to force radical changes on the entire country.\nSome claim that you want to force everyone to eat a vegetarian diet and make private\ntransport illegal.\n你这本书的批评者断言，你想把激进的改变强加给整个国家。有人声称你想强迫所有人吃素\n食、把私人交通工具变成非法。\n【信号】Critics assert 转述信号。出现 Critics of your book assert that 这一转述信号，明确告诉\n你下面就是批评者的说法，正对题干的 critics，是答案句标志。\n【替换】you're trying to force radical changes on the entire country ↔\nShe tries to force people to accept her radical ideas.\n几近同词的核心保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\nShe neglects people's efforts in animal protection. 提到 animal protection 蹭了动物话\n题，但批评者从没说她「忽视他人的动保努力」，原文无此评价。\nC) 原词陷阱\nShe ignores the various benefits of public transport. 用了反复出现的 public\ntransport，但批评者根本没说她「无视公共交通的好处」——恰恰相反，她是支持公共交\n通的，这是把话题词乱拼成的指控。\n借⽤原⽂：make private transport illegal\nD) 张冠李戴\nShe insists vegetarians are harming the environment. 用了 vegetarian 和\nenvironment 两个高频词，但批评者说的是她想强迫大家吃素，绝非说「素食者在破坏环\n境」，意思被拧反了。\n借⽤原⽂：you want to force everyone to eat a vegetarian diet", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman claim about the diet changes she suggested?", "options": ["A) They are significant.", "B) They are revolutionary.", "C) They are rational.", "D) They are modest."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are modest.\n【定位】In my guidelines for saving the environment, I suggest modest changes, like eating vegetarian\nmeals two days a week.\n在我那份保护环境的指南里，我建议的是温和的改变，比如每周吃两天素食。\n【信号】I suggest+定性词。她亲口说 I suggest modest changes，suggest 后紧跟的形容词就是\n她对所建议改变的定性，是这题答案信号。\n【替换】I suggest modest changes ↔ They are modest.\n同词直接还原\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are significant. 说重大，与她自述的 modest(温和)程度相反；她特意强调改变不\n大，significant 正好拧反。\n借⽤原⽂：I suggest modest changes\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey are revolutionary. 革命性，呼应了前文批评者说的 radical，但那是批评者的指控，\n她本人恰恰否认并改称 modest，把批评口吻当成她的定性就上当了。\n借⽤原⽂：force radical changes\nC) 未提及\nThey are rational. 理性的，听起来像褒义合理，但录音里她用的词是 modest 而非\nrational，原文从未称这些改变「理性」。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about her suggested tax increase?", "options": ["A) It would help to protect the environment.", "B) It would generate money for public health.", "C) It would need support from the general public.", "D) It would force poor people to change their diet."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It would help to protect the environment.\n【定位】But the taxes I suggest aren't that high, less than 3% only. Plus, the money generated would\nbe allocated to environmental protection, which benefits everyone.\n但我建议的税率没那么高，只有不到3%。而且，所产生的钱会被用于环境保护，这对每个\n人都有益。\n【信号】Plus 之后补出关键好处。她先用 But 澄清税不高，再用 Plus, the money generated\nwould be allocated to environmental protection 补出加税的去向与好处，Plus 引出的\n这句才是她想强调的作用，是答案句。\n【替换】the money generated would be allocated to environmental protection ↔\nIt would help to protect the environment.\n措施钱去向=保护环境\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIt would generate money for public health. 借了原文 the money generated，但钱的\n去向是 environmental protection(环保)而非 public health(公共卫生)，把用途偷换了。\n借⽤原⽂：the money generated would be allocated to environmental protection\nC) 未提及\nIt would need support from the general public. 听着合理，但她谈加税时只说税率低、\n钱用于环保，从没说这措施「需要公众支持」，是无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt would force poor people to change their diet. 含 force 和 poor people，但这是主持\n人质疑时的说法(第9段)，正是她要反驳的，不是她自己对加税的说法，题干问的是她怎么\n说。\n借⽤原⽂：Those taxes could force poor people to adopt your life choices.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What has the woman revealed in her book?", "options": ["A) Where successful people's strengths come from.", "B) Why many people fight so hard for success.", "C) How she achieved her life's goal.", "D) What makes people successful."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） What makes people successful.\n【定位】She claims to have uncovered how people achieve success.\n她声称揭示了人们是如何获得成功的。\n【信号】开头点题句。主持人开场介绍时一句 She claims to have uncovered how people achieve\nsuccess. 直接交代了全书主题，开头点题正是主旨题的核心定位信号。\n【替换】how people achieve success ↔ What makes people successful.\n\"如何获得成功\"=\"什么造就成功\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确说成功者 they know their strengths when pursuing a goal，A 把 strengths 单拎\n出来问 \"strengths 从哪来\"，但 \"strengths 的来源\" 录音根本没讲，且它只是众多特征之\n一，不是全书主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：they know their strengths when pursuing a goal\nB) 未提及\nB 问 \"为什么很多人拼命追求成功\"（原因），但全篇谈的是成功者 \"有哪些共同特征\"，从未\n讨论 \"人为何要拼命求成功\" 这个动机问题，属无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说 \"她（作者）如何实现人生目标\"，但录音里这位女士是在 \"揭示别人成功的规律\"，谈的\n是 people / successful people，不是讲她自己的成功经历，主语张冠李戴。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do successful people attribute their achievements to?", "options": ["A) Having someone who has confidence in them.", "B) Having someone who is ready to help them.", "C) Having a firm belief in their own ability.", "D) Having a realistic attitude towards life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Having someone who has confidence in them.\n【定位】They credit their success to having someone in their life who believes in them.\n他们把成功归功于生命中有一个相信他们的人。\n【信号】归因信号 credit...to。被问者（女士）的应答 They credit their success to having\nsomeone in their life who believes in them. 中 credit...to（归功于）正是归因信号，它\n后面跟的就是答案点。\n【替换】someone in their life who believes in them ↔ Having someone who has confidence in them.\nbelieves in=has confidence in\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB \"有人随时愿帮他们\" 与 A 仅差核心词。录音说的是 someone who believes in them\n（相信），不是 \"愿意帮忙\"。\"愿帮忙\" 是后文 people will be willing to help them 才提\n到、且属另一道题的内容，搬到这里就答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：people will be willing to help them\nC) 偷换概念\nC \"对自身能力有坚定信念\" 把主语换成了 \"自己\"，而录音明确是 someone...who believes\nin them——是 \"别人相信他\"，主语方向相反。\nD) 未提及\nD \"对生活持现实态度\" 在整段归因表述里完全没出现，录音只说归功于 \"有人相信他们\"，\n与 \"现实态度\" 无关，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do successful people do when faced with difficulties?", "options": ["A) They adjust their goals accordingly.", "B) They try hard to appear optimistic.", "C) They stay positive.", "D) They remain calm."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They stay positive.\n【定位】Successful people are also optimistic as it is important to stay positive while being aware of\nobstacles that can deter us from achieving our goals.\n成功者也很乐观，因为在意识到那些会阻碍我们达成目标的障碍的同时，保持积极是很重\n要的。\n【信号】困难词 obstacles 附近的描述。被问者说 it is important to stay positive while being\naware of obstacles，obstacles（障碍）这一困难词附近，紧跟的 stay positive 正是成功\n者面对困难的反应。\n【替换】stay positive ↔ They stay positive.\n原话直接对应\nSuccessful people are also optimistic ↔ stay positive\noptimistic 与 positive 同义\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA \"相应调整目标\"，但录音第 5 段说成功者 don't see obstacles as something that\nprevents success, but mere inconveniences that need to be overcome——是 \"克服障\n碍\" 而非 \"改目标\"，方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：mere inconveniences that need to be overcome\nB) 偷换概念\nB \"努力让自己显得乐观\"，录音确说 optimistic、stay positive，但那是 \"真的保持积极\"，\n不是 \"appear（装作）乐观\"，selflessly 加了一个 \"装样子\" 的意思，曲解原意。\n借⽤原⽂：Successful people are also optimistic\nD) 偷换概念\nD \"remain calm（保持冷静）\" 与 C \"stay positive（保持积极）\" 极易混，但录音说的是\npositive / optimistic（积极乐观），从未说 calm（冷静），用近义词偷梁换柱。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What is one prerequisite for passion at work according to the woman?", "options": ["A) An understanding leadership.", "B) A nurturing environment.", "C) Mutual respect among colleagues.", "D) Highly cooperative teammates."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A nurturing environment.\n【定位】There's a prerequisite, that is, you have to work in an environment that nurtures passion.\n有一个前提条件，那就是，你必须在一个能培育激情的环境里工作。\n【信号】明示信号 prerequisite + have to。被问者直接说 There's a prerequisite, that is, you\nhave to work in an environment that nurtures passion.，prerequisite（前提）加 have\nto（必须）双重 \"必需\" 信号，答案点就在其后。\n【替换】an environment that nurtures passion ↔ A nurturing environment.\n动词短语压缩成名词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA \"an understanding leadership\"，前提句只讲 \"培育激情的环境\"，全程未提 \"领导\"；\nunderstanding 一词倒是在别处 understanding the needs of others 出现过、但那是讲\n\"理解他人需求\" 的合作，与领导无关。\n借⽤原⽂：understanding the needs of others\nC) 原词陷阱\nC \"同事间相互尊重\"，mutual respect 确在录音 this often leads to a great deal of\nmutual respect 出现过，但那是讲合作 \"带来\" 的结果、且属第 6-7 题语境，不是 \"激情的\n前提\"，搬来此处答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：this often leads to a great deal of mutual respect\nD) 张冠李戴\nD \"highly cooperative teammates\"，录音确说成功者 good at cooperating with\npeople，但那是讲成功者自身的合作能力，不是 \"激情的前提\"；把合作内容嫁接到前提题\n上属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：good at cooperating with people", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about mega bats?", "options": ["A) They use their sense of hearing to capture their prey.", "B) Their food mainly consists of small animals and fish.", "C) They have big eyes and distinctive visual centers.", "D) Their ancestor is different from that of micro bats."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They have big eyes and distinctive visual centers.\n【定位】These species have distinctive visual centers and big eyes.\n这些物种拥有发达独特的视觉中枢和大眼睛。\n【信号】对象+特征的直陈句。录音在介绍 mega bats 后紧接一句专讲其生理特征 These species\nhave distinctive visual centers and big eyes.，这种紧贴对象的直陈描述句就是承载特征\n题答案的句子。\n【替换】distinctive visual centers and big eyes ↔ big eyes and distinctive visual centers\n同内容换语序\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音说 mega bats 是 They use senses of vision and smell to capture their prey.，靠视\n觉和嗅觉捕食；A 把别处提到的 sense of hearing 安到 mega bats 头上，且 mega 用的根\n本不是听觉。\n借⽤原⽂：They use senses of vision and smell to capture their prey.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 eat fruits, flowers, and sometimes small animals or fish，B 盗用了 small\nanimals and fish 这几个原词，但只截取了一部分（漏了 fruits, flowers）、把偶尔吃说成\n主食，是以偏概全的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：eat fruits, flowers, and sometimes small animals or fish\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说两类蝙蝠 both from a common ancestor，是共同祖先；D 称 mega 的祖先与\nmicro 不同，正好与原文相反，是把同变成了不同。\n借⽤原⽂：both from a common ancestor", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How do micro bats find their way and identify food?", "options": ["A) With the help of moonlight.", "B) By means of echolocation.", "C) With the aid of daylight vision.", "D) By means of vision and smell."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） By means of echolocation.\n【定位】These species use echolocation to find their way and identify food.\n这些物种利用回声定位来找路和辨别食物。\n【信号】方式动词use直陈手段。录音用 use echolocation to find their way and identify food 把\n手段（echolocation）和目的（find way / identify food）一句说全，use 后接的就是\nHow 题要的方式，是标准答案句。\n【替换】use echolocation ↔ By means of echolocation\n动词改介词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 moonlight 出现在讲 mega bats 的 cannot fly during the night with no\nmoonlight，是说没月光就飞不了，既不是 micro bats、又是个否定语境；A 盗\nmoonlight 这个原词答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：cannot fly during the night with no moonlight\nC) 张冠李戴\ndaylight vision 是录音描述 mega bats（Flying Foxes）的 rely on their daylight\nvision，C 把 mega 的本领安到 micro bats 的找路方式上，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：rely on their daylight vision\nD) 张冠李戴\nvision and smell 出自 mega bats 的 They use senses of vision and smell to capture\ntheir prey.，是 mega 的捕食手段；D 把这套手段挪给 micro bats 找路，盗原词又张冠李\n戴。\n借⽤原⽂：They use senses of vision and smell to capture their prey.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why do some species of bats use their sense of hearing more than their eyes?", "options": ["A) To make up for their natural absence of vision.", "B) To adapt themselves to a particular lifestyle.", "C) To facilitate their travel over long distances.", "D) To survive in the ever-changing weather."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To adapt themselves to a particular lifestyle.\n【定位】Some species use their sense of hearing more than their eyes as a matter of adaptation to a\nparticular lifestyle, but their eyes are still functional.\n有些物种更多地依赖听觉而非眼睛，这是对某种特定生活方式的一种适应，但它们的眼睛依\n然是有功能的。\n【信号】as a matter of点出原因。末段用 as a matter of adaptation to a particular lifestyle\n直接给出更依赖听觉的缘由，as a matter of 是显式的原因信号，这句就是 Why 题的答\n案句。\n【替换】as a matter of adaptation to a particular lifestyle ↔ To adapt themselves to a particular lifestyle\n名词化改不定式\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音末段明确 there are no bats which are naturally blind、眼睛仍 functional；A 称要\n弥补天生没视力，与原文 naturally blind 的否定恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：there are no bats which are naturally blind\nC) 原词陷阱\nlong distances 出自第三段 vision is used by micro bats to travel over long\ndistances，说的是视觉的用途；C 盗这个原词来回答更依赖听觉的原因，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：vision is used by micro bats to travel over long distances\nD) 未提及\n录音从未把更依赖听觉归因于应对天气，weather 一词全篇没出现；D 是凭空添加的原因，\n属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say about children watching educational programs?", "options": ["A) They acquire knowledge not found in books.", "B) They learn how to interact with their peers.", "C) They become more emotionally aggressive.", "D) They get much better prepared for school."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They become more emotionally aggressive.\n【定位】Children who spend more time watching educational programs increase their emotional\naggression toward other children.\n花更多时间看教育类节目的孩子，会增加他们对其他孩子的情绪攻击性。\n【信号】话题词定位 + 反预期的负面定性。录音开头说研究发现教育节目\"comes with an added\nlesson that influences the child's behavior\"，随即点出 increase their emotional\naggression toward other children——这个\"与直觉相反的负面结果\"正是研究要讲的核\n心，是答案句的信号。\n【替换】increase their emotional aggression toward other children ↔\nbecome more emotionally aggressive\n动宾结构改写为系表结构\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实反复说 educational、added lesson，They acquire knowledge not found in\nbooks. 顺势编出\"学到书本外的知识\"，但 learn the educational lesson that was\nintended 说的是\"学到了节目本意要教的内容\"，并非\"书本里没有的知识\"，属听到 lesson\n就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：learn the educational lesson that was intended\nB) 未提及\nThey learn how to interact with their peers.\"学会与同伴相处\"听起来很像教育节目的好\n处，但录音通篇讲的是孩子对其他孩子\"increase emotional aggression\"，恰恰是相处变\n差，从未说学会良性互动，属未提及。\nD) 过度推断\nThey get much better prepared for school. 与录音 learn to watch the show for those\nmessages 的方向无关，录音从没说看节目让孩子\"更适应学校\"；它借\"教育\"联想到\"上\n学\"，是凭常识推过头的正向结论。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why can't preschool children get the moral conveyed in the TV programs?", "options": ["A) They are far from emotionally prepared.", "B) They tend to be more attracted by images.", "C) They can't follow the conflicts in the show.", "D) They lack the cognitive and memory skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They lack the cognitive and memory skills.\n【定位】That's because it requires that they understand how all the parts of the show fit together. You\nneed pretty complicated cognitive skills and memory skills to be able to do that. These are\nstill developing in young children.\n那是因为这要求他们理解节目所有部分如何拼接在一起。要做到这点，你需要相当复杂的认\n知能力和记忆能力。而这些能力在幼儿身上还在发育之中。\n【信号】因果信号 That's because 直接领出原因。录音先说 Preschool children really don't get\nthe moral of the story，紧接 That's because it requires that they understand how all\nthe parts of the show fit together——That's because 是明确的因果信号，其后两句给\n出真正原因：需要复杂的认知与记忆能力，而幼儿这些能力还在发育。\n【替换】You need pretty complicated cognitive skills and memory skills ↔\nThey lack the cognitive and memory skills.\n\"需要却还在发育\"=\"缺乏\"的肯否定转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 emotional 多次出现（emotional aggression、emotionally aggressive\ntactics），They are far from emotionally prepared. 借这个 emotionally 编出\"情绪上远\n未准备好\"，但录音解释孩子领会不了寓意，归因的是认知记忆能力，而非情绪准备，属听\n到 emotionally 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：they may use emotionally aggressive tactics with each other\nB) 未提及\nThey tend to be more attracted by images.\"更易被画面吸引\"是关于幼儿看电视的常识印\n象，但录音解释\"领会不了寓意\"时根本没提画面或图像，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 the portrayal of conflict in media 与角色间冲突的描述，They can't follow the\nconflicts in the show. 借 conflict 编出\"跟不上剧中冲突\"；但录音说孩子缺的是把\"all the\nparts fit together\"整体串联的能力，不是单看不懂冲突这一点，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：it requires that they understand how all the parts of the show fit together", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the passage suggest parents do?", "options": ["A) Choose appropriate programs for their children.", "B) Help their children understand the program's plot.", "C) Outline the program's plot for their children first.", "D) Monitor their children's watching of TV programs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Help their children understand the program's plot.\n【定位】Parents should instead watch with their kids and help them to understand the plot.\n父母反而应该和孩子一起看，并帮助他们理解剧情。\n【信号】建议信号 should + 转折 instead。录音先说 parents shouldn't completely constrain\nchildren's viewing（不该完全限制孩子观看），紧接 Parents should instead watch with\ntheir kids and help them to understand the plot——should 加 instead 的组合，正\n是\"否定旧做法、给出新建议\"的强信号，instead 后才是真正建议。\n【替换】help them to understand the plot ↔ Help their children understand the program's plot.\n代词还原 + 几乎同义复述\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 program/programs 多次出现，Choose appropriate programs for their children.\n借此编出\"为孩子挑合适的节目\"，但录音的建议是陪看并帮孩子理解，从没说\"挑选节目\"，\n属听到 programs 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Parents should instead watch with their kids and help them to understand the plot\nC) 原词陷阱\nOutline the program's plot for their children first. 借录音里的 plot 编出\"先给孩子概述\n情节\"，但录音说的是\"帮孩子理解情节（help them understand the plot）\"，是陪着理\n解，而非父母先单方面 outline，属原词陷阱加偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：help them to understand the plot\nD) 与原文相反\nMonitor their children's watching of TV programs.\"监督孩子看电视\"听着像合理建议，\n但录音明说 parents shouldn't completely constrain children's viewing——反对单纯管\n控，主张陪看引导。D 取的恰是录音否定掉的方向，属与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：parents shouldn't completely constrain children's viewing", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What should parents do right after watching the TV program?", "options": ["A) Explain its message to their children.", "B) Check if their children have enjoyed it.", "C) Encourage their children to retell the story.", "D) Ask their children to describe its characters."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Explain its message to their children.\n【定位】Parents can comment along the way and then explain the message at the end.\n父母可以在观看过程中随时点评，然后在结尾时解释其寓意。\n【信号】时间对照 along the way 对 at the end。录音把父母的动作按时间分两段：comment\nalong the way（看的过程中点评）与 explain the message at the end（在结尾解释寓\n意）。题干问的是 right after（看完之后），对应的正是 at the end 这一时点的动作，时\n间标志 at the end 是定位信号。\n【替换】explain the message at the end ↔ Explain its message to their children.\n时间状语换补语，动宾近乎照搬\n【排除】B) 未提及\nCheck if their children have enjoyed it.\"看孩子是否喜欢\"是看完节目后家长常做的常识动\n作，但录音讲看完后是\"explain the message（解释寓意）\"，从未提\"问喜不喜欢\"，属未\n提及。\nC) 偷换概念\nEncourage their children to retell the story. 与录音相近的是\"看完后做点什么\"，但录音\n的动作是父母\"explain the message（解释寓意）\"，是父母讲给孩子，不是\"鼓励孩子复\n述\"，主语和动作都被偷换，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：explain the message at the end\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音结尾说父母要 explain how certain types of behavior were not appropriate（解释\n某些行为为何不当），Ask their children to describe its characters. 借\"行为/角色\"的联\n想编出\"让孩子描述角色\"，但录音是父母讲解行为是否得当，不是让孩子描述人物，属原词\n陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：explain how certain types of behavior were not appropriate", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Why don't many Westerners take off shoes before entering a house?", "options": ["A) They are afraid of injuring their feet.", "B) They have never developed the habit.", "C) They believe a little dirt harms no one.", "D) They find it rather troublesome to do so."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They believe a little dirt harms no one.\n【定位】For many in those countries, wiping their shoes before going inside is regarded as sufficient.\nAfter all, they may think a little dirt doesn't hurt anyone.\n对那些国家的许多人来说，进门前把鞋蹭一蹭就算够了。毕竟，他们也许觉得一点脏东西伤\n不了谁。\n【信号】After all 引出真实想法。答案落在 After all, they may think a little dirt doesn't hurt\nanyone. 这句，信号词是 After all——它引出 \"人们之所以不脱鞋\" 背后的真实想法，是这\n道原因题的落点。\n【替换】a little dirt doesn't hurt anyone ↔ a little dirt harms no one\n同义改写：hurt anyone = harm no one\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey are afraid of injuring their feet. 盗用了原文 incur foot injuries 这个 \"脚受伤\" 的字\n眼，但原文那句是说 \"穿鞋越久越容易伤脚\"，是赤脚有益的论据，根本不是 \"西方人不脱鞋\n的原因\"，属听到 \"伤脚\" 就误选。\n借⽤原⽂：the more likely you are to incur foot injuries\nB)\nThey have never developed the habit. 听上去合理，但原文只说脱鞋 has long been a\npractice in Asian countries、西方人 have yet to embrace the no-shoes-in-the-house\nrule，是在陈述 \"做不做\" 的现状，从未把 \"没养成习惯\" 点为他们不脱鞋的原因，属原文未\n明说的推断。\n借⽤原⽂：many people in Western countries have yet to embrace the no-shoes-in-the-house rule\nD)\nThey find it rather troublesome to do so. 在原文里找不到对应——说话人只说他们觉得\n蹭蹭鞋 is regarded as sufficient（就够了），从没说他们 \"嫌脱鞋麻烦\"，这是凭生活常识\n脑补出来的，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is the finding of one study by researchers at an American university?", "options": ["A) Different types of bacteria existed on public-toilet floors.", "B) There were more bacteria on sidewalks than in the home.", "C) Office carpets collected more bacteria than elsewhere.", "D) A large number of bacteria collected on a single shoe."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A large number of bacteria collected on a single shoe.\n【定位】In one study conducted at an American university, researchers collected microscopic germs\nfrom footwear. They found that up to 421,000 units of bacteria can collect on the outside of a\nsingle shoe.\n在美国一所大学开展的一项研究中，研究人员从鞋上采集了微观细菌。他们发现，一只鞋的\n外侧最多可聚集多达 42.1 万个单位的细菌。\n【信号】They found 引出研究结论。信号是 In one study conducted at an American university\n点出研究，紧接 They found that up to 421,000 units of bacteria can collect on the\noutside of a single shoe 用 \"They found\" 抛出具体发现——一只鞋上多达 42.1 万单位细\n菌，这就是答案句。\n【替换】up to 421,000 units of bacteria can collect on the outside of a single shoe ↔\nA large number of bacteria collected on a single shoe\n具体数字概括为 a large number\n【排除】A)\nDifferent types of bacteria existed on public-toilet floors. 盗用原文 public toilet\nfloors 这个原词，但那句 They collect on your shoes when you walk along sidewalks,\npublic toilet floors and even office carpets 是泛说 \"细菌哪里都有\"，并非那项研究的发\n现；且 \"不同种类的细菌\" 原文也没提，属张冠李戴加无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：public toilet floors\nB)\nThere were more bacteria on sidewalks than in the home. 盗用 sidewalks 一词，但原\n文只把人行道与公厕、办公室地毯并列说 \"都会沾菌\"，从没拿 \"人行道\" 和 \"家中\" 作\nmore…than 的比较，这个比较是凭空加上去的。\n借⽤原⽂：when you walk along sidewalks, public toilet floors and even office carpets\nC)\nOffice carpets collected more bacteria than elsewhere. 盗用原文 office carpets，但原\n文只把办公室地毯当作 \"也会沾菌\" 的并列地点之一，还用 even 表示 \"连地毯都沾\"，绝没\n有说它 \"比别处收集得多\"，属偷换为最高级/比较级。\n借⽤原⽂：and even office carpets", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the final reason the speaker gives for removing shoes before entering a house?", "options": ["A) The chemicals on shoes can deteriorate air quality.", "B) Shoes can upset family members with their noise.", "C) The marks left by shoes are hard to erase.", "D) Shoes can leave scratches on the floor."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The chemicals on shoes can deteriorate air quality.\n【定位】And if people aren't yet convinced by my arguments, I can give one final reason. The dust and\ntoxic chemicals you bring into your house via your shoes can deteriorate the air quality in\nyour home.\n如果有人还没被我的论据说服，我可以再给出最后一个理由。你通过鞋子带进家里的灰尘和\n有毒化学物质，会让家中的空气质量变差。\n【信号】one final reason 标志收尾理由。信号是 I can give one final reason——\"one final\nreason\" 明确宣告这是最后一个理由，紧跟其后的 The dust and toxic chemicals you\nbring into your house via your shoes can deteriorate the air quality in your home 即\n答案句。\n【替换】toxic chemicals you bring into your house via your shoes can deteriorate the air quality ↔\nThe chemicals on shoes can deteriorate air quality\n几乎同义、deteriorate 原样保留\n【排除】B)\nShoes can upset family members with their noise. 盗用第 2 段 removing your noisy\nshoes is the polite thing to do 里的 \"noisy/噪音\" 概念，但原文那是说为楼下邻居着想，\n针对的是 downstairs neighbors 而非 family members，且它出现在中间、不是 \"最后一\n个\" 理由，属张冠李戴加位置错位。\n借⽤原⽂：removing your noisy shoes is the polite thing to do out of consideration for your\ndownstairs neighbors\nC) 未提及\nThe marks left by shoes are hard to erase. 盗用第 2 段 leave marks and scratches on\nfloors 的 \"marks\"，但原文只说鞋会留下痕迹划痕、会损伤地板，从没说这些痕迹 \"难以清\n除\"，\"hard to erase\" 是脑补；而且这属第 2 段的地板理由，不是最后理由。\n借⽤原⽂：Shoes can also leave marks and scratches on floors\nD)\nShoes can leave scratches on the floor. 几乎照搬第 2 段原词 leave marks and\nscratches on floors，内容原文确实说了，但它是中间列举的地板理由之一，不是题目要的\n\"one final reason\"，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Shoes can also leave marks and scratches on floors, especially high heels", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about hiding one's emotions?", "options": ["A) It is sinful and immoral.", "B) It is deemed uncivilized.", "C) It is an uncontrollable behavior.", "D) It is a violation of faith and trust."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It is a violation of faith and trust.\n【定位】Socially, it may be a good tactic to hide our true emotions, but morally it is also a breach of\nfaith and trust.\n在社交上，隐藏真实情绪也许是个不错的策略，但在道德上，它也是一种对信任的背弃。\n【信号】转折 but + morally 后才是真意。前半 Socially, it may be a good tactic 是退一步的让\n步，真正的定性在 but morally it is also a breach of faith and trust，转折词 but 与立场\n状语 morally 之后那句才是说话人的态度落点。\n【替换】a breach of faith and trust ↔ a violation of faith and trust\nbreach = violation 近义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt is sinful and immoral. 借用了第二段 There is nothing sinful or immoral about being\nemotional 里的原词，但那是在说\"有情绪本身\"并不可耻，且原文用的是 nothing... 的否\n定，与\"隐藏情绪\"无关。\n借⽤原⽂：There is nothing sinful or immoral about being emotional\nB) 未提及\nIt is deemed uncivilized. 说\"被视为不文明\"，但录音通篇没把隐藏情绪定性为\"不文明\"，\n属凭常识臆测的无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt is an uncontrollable behavior. 的\"控制\"主题来自第二、三段讲情绪如何被理性脑调控，\n与\"隐藏情绪是何性质\"无关，且原文从未说隐藏情绪\"无法控制\"。\n借⽤原⽂：the rational part of the mind controls the information", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What should we do first in dealing with emotions?", "options": ["A) Assess their consequences.", "B) Guard against their harm.", "C) Accept them as normal.", "D) Find out their causes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Accept them as normal.\n【定位】One of the first steps in dealing with emotions such as anger or fear is to acknowledge them\nas normal and human.\n处理愤怒或恐惧这类情绪的第一步，是承认它们是正常的、属于人之常情的。\n【信号】首步标志 one of the first steps。One of the first steps in dealing with emotions 直接\n对应题干的 do first，是明确的首步信号，其后的 to acknowledge them as normal and\nhuman 就是要做的第一步动作。\n【替换】to acknowledge them as normal and human ↔ Accept them as normal.\nacknowledge≈accept、保留 as normal\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nAssess their consequences. 中的\"后果\"虽与第二段 more devastating and damaging 的\n语气沾边，但原文从未把\"评估后果\"列为第一步，是借语气制造的答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：more devastating and damaging than the experience of emotion itself\nB) 与原文相反\nGuard against their harm. 主张\"提防情绪的危害\"，但本段恰恰说\"有情绪没什么可耻\"、要\n接纳，立场相反，并非要防备情绪。\n借⽤原⽂：acknowledge them as normal and human\nD) 未提及\nFind out their causes. 说找出情绪的成因，录音里并未把\"找原因\"列为处理情绪的第一步，\n属凭常识联想的无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What are we advised to do when emotions arise?", "options": ["A) Try to understand what messages they convey.", "B) Pay attention to their possible consequences.", "C) Consider them from different perspectives.", "D) Make sure they are brought under control."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Try to understand what messages they convey.\n【定位】When emotions arise, instead of stifling them, pay attention to them and try to understand the\nmessages they are trying to deliver.\n当情绪升起时，不要压抑它们，而要留意它们，并试着理解它们想要传达的信息。\n【信号】建议信号 instead of...pay attention to。情境词 When emotions arise 与题干完全对\n应，紧跟的 instead of stifling them, pay attention to them and try to understand the\nmessages they are trying to deliver 是明确建议句，instead of（而不是）排除了\"压\n抑\"、点出真正主张。\n【替换】try to understand the messages they are trying to deliver ↔\nTry to understand what messages they convey.\ndeliver=convey、句式微调\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nPay attention to their possible consequences. 用了 pay attention to（原文确有 pay\nattention to them），却把宾语偷换成 consequences —— 原文要留意的是情绪本身/其\n信息，不是后果。\n借⽤原⽂：pay attention to them\nC) 未提及\nConsider them from different perspectives. 说\"从不同角度看待\"，录音末段并无\"多角\n度\"之说，属听起来不错但原文没有的无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nMake sure they are brought under control. 借了全文反复出现的 control，但末段恰恰主\n张 instead of stifling them —— 不要压制、而要倾听，方向正相反。\n借⽤原⽂：instead of stifling them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is an urgent need for feeding the world's population in 2050, according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Cultivation of new varieties of crops.", "B) Measures to cope with climate change.", "C) Development of more effective pesticides.", "D) Application of more nitrogen-rich fertilizers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Cultivation of new varieties of crops.\n【定位】There is an urgent need for new crop varieties.\n迫切需要新的作物品种。\n【信号】urgent need 强调信号。题干钩子词在第二段原句 There is an urgent need for new\ncrop varieties 里几乎原样出现,urgent need 这一强调结构直接顶出了答案对象 new crop\nvarieties,这就是答案句信号。\n【替换】new crop varieties ↔ Cultivation of new varieties of crops\n同概念换序改写\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n录音确实说新品种要 more resistant to drought, heat and pests(抗旱、抗热、抗虫),B\n把\"抗热\"过度引申成 Measures to cope with climate change(应对气候变化),拔高了原文,\n录音并未把\"应对气候变化\"列为急需。\n借⽤原⽂：more resistant to drought, heat and pests\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 pesticides(农药)是借录音 more resistant to ... pests 里的 pests(害虫)来诱选,但录音\n讲的是\"新品种本身要抗虫\",根本没提\"研发更有效的农药\",方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：more resistant to drought, heat and pests\nD) 与原文相反\nD 直接盗用录音原词 nitrogen-rich fertilizers,但录音说新品种要 use less water,\nnitrogen-rich fertilizers or other inputs(少用水、少用氮肥),D 却说 Application of more\nnitrogen-rich fertilizers(施更多氮肥),把\"少用\"反成了\"多用\"。\n借⽤原⽂：use less water, nitrogen-rich fertilizers or other inputs", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Where should most of the money be invested to feed the ever-growing population?", "options": ["A) The expansion of farmland in developing countries.", "B) The research on crop rotation in developing countries.", "C) The cooperation of the world's agricultural scientists.", "D) The improvement of agricultural infrastructure."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The improvement of agricultural infrastructure.\n【定位】Most of that money needs to go towards improving agricultural infrastructure.\n那笔钱的大部分需要用于改善农业基础设施。\n【信号】Most of money go towards 直陈投向。第三段原句 Most of that money needs to go\ntowards improving agricultural infrastructure 与题干 most of the money 措辞对应,go\ntowards 直接交代了钱的去向,是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】improving agricultural infrastructure ↔ The improvement of agricultural infrastructure\n动词名词化改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 借 Developing nations 这个原词造\"发展中国家\"的熟悉感,但录音讲的是发展中国家\"更\n好地运用现代技术\"来增产,从未提 expansion of farmland(扩大耕地),属无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：Developing nations could score substantial gains in productivity by making better use\nof modern technologies and practices\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 的 crop rotation 盗自第二段 crop rotation and mixed farming,那是讲\"需要低技术研究\n的基础项\",和本题\"钱投在哪\"无关;B 把跨段细节硬拼成投资对象,答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：crop rotation and mixed farming of animals and plants on small farms\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 的 cooperation of the world's agricultural scientists 来自第四段 the world's\nagricultural scientists are embracing such a broad view 一带的内容,讲的是科学家联手\n开会,与本题\"钱主要投哪\"是两码事,张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：the world's agricultural scientists are embracing such a broad view", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why does the speaker give credit to the world's agricultural scientists?", "options": ["A) For encouraging farmers to embrace new farming techniques.", "B) For aligning their research with advances in farming technology.", "C) For turning their focus to the needs of farmers in poorer countries.", "D) For cooperating closely with policymakers in developing countries."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） For turning their focus to the needs of farmers in poorer countries.\n【定位】To their credit, the world's agricultural scientists are embracing such a broad view. In March,\nfor example, they came together at the first Global Conference on Agricultural Research to\nbegin working out how to change research agendas to help meet the needs of farmers in\npoorer nations.\n值得称道的是,全球农业科学家们正在接受这样一种宽阔的视野。比如今年三月,他们齐聚首\n届全球农业研究大会,着手研究如何调整研究议程,以帮助满足较贫困国家农民的需求。\n【信号】To their credit 表扬信号引出原因。第四段以 To their credit(值得称道的是)这一肯定信号\n开头,紧跟 came together at the first Global Conference(齐聚大会)交代了被表扬的具体\n做法——科学家联手合作,这正是答案理由。\n【替换】they came together at the first Global Conference on Agricultural Research ↔\nThe cooperation of the world's agricultural scientists\n具体行为概括为合作\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 new farming techniques 让人联想第二段讲新品种/新技术,但录音从未说科学家\"鼓励\n农民采纳新技术\";被表扬的是科学家联手开会,A 是无中生有的理由。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 盗用录音 change research agendas(调整研究议程)制造熟悉感,但录音说调整议程是\"为\n满足穷国农民需求\",B 却说成 aligning their research with advances in farming\ntechnology(让研究对齐农业技术进步),偷换了目的。\n借⽤原⽂：change research agendas to help meet the needs of farmers in poorer nations\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借第四段末 policy-makers 原词诱选,但录音说的是 these plans will not bear fruit\nunless they get considerably more support from policy-makers(除非获得政策制定者\n更多支持,计划才能见效)——这是\"尚缺的条件\",而非科学家已做、被表扬的事,方向错位。\n借⽤原⽂：these plans will not bear fruit unless they get considerably more support from\npolicy-makers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What makes China exceptional in comparison with the rest of the world?", "options": ["A) Rapid transition to become a food exporter.", "B) Substantial funding in agricultural research.", "C) Quick rise to become a leading grain producer.", "D) Assumption of humanitarian responsibilities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Substantial funding in agricultural research.\n【定位】The big exception is China, where spending has far surpassed other countries over the\npast decade.\n最大的例外是中国,过去十年里中国的(农业研究)支出远远超过其他国家。\n【信号】The big exception 例外信号。第五段 The big exception is China 用 exception 与题干\nexceptional 直接呼应,紧跟 spending has far surpassed other countries 交代了中国例外\n之处——研究支出远超他国,这就是答案句。\n【替换】spending has far surpassed other countries ↔ Substantial funding in agricultural research\n支出远超=大量投入\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音讲中国 transition to become the key supplier of relevant science and\ntechnology(转为向穷国供应相关科技),A 把 supplier/transition 误读成 transition to\nbecome a food exporter(转为粮食出口国),录音从没说中国成为粮食出口国。\n借⽤原⽂：transition to become the key supplier of relevant science and technology to\npoorer countries\nC) 未提及\nC 的 leading grain producer(领先产粮国)蹭了全篇\"粮食/农业\"的主题词,但录音讲中国例\n外是因为研究\"支出\"远超他国,完全没说中国是产粮大国,无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 盗用录音 humanitarian responsibility 原词,但那句是 developed countries have a\nhumanitarian responsibility too(发达国家也有人道责任),主语是\"发达国家\",不是中国;D 把\n这责任安到中国头上,张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：developed countries have a humanitarian responsibility too", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) align\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列谓语，需第三人称单数动词\n【依据】align with=与……契合\nalign with 是地道搭配，意为\"与……一致/契合\"，clothes that fit well and align with\nyour sense of style\"既合身又契合你的审美\"——与上文 ill-fitting/over- or under-\ndressed 形成正反对照，自然地引出 can improve your confidence。\n【竞争词】N) showcase 搭配不通、语义偏\"展示\"\nshowcase 通常是\"showcase sth\"（及物动词，直接接宾语），不与 with 搭配；showcase\nwith your sense of style 既不合搭配也不合语义（衣服并非\"借由你的审美进行展示\"）。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 并列谓语，需第三人称单数动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) current\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词 clothing\n【依据】\"现有的衣服\"与下句\"买全新一套\"形成对照\n本段在问\"能不能不买新衣，靠**现有**衣服来改善健康\"——improve your health\nthrough your current clothing, without having to dash out and buy a whole new\nwardrobe，current\"现有的\"与下半句 a whole new 构成强对比，逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】F) fond 搭配错位、语义偏\"喜爱\"\nfond 作\"喜爱\"用时多为 be fond of；作\"温馨深情的\"通常修饰 memories/associations，\n与\"clothing\"直接搭配不自然，且\"喜爱的衣服\"与下句\"全新一套\"的对照逻辑也没有\ncurrent 顺。\nM) reluctant 感情色彩与对象不符\nreluctant\"不情愿的\"通常修饰人，不修饰物品；\"不情愿的衣服\"语义错位，逻辑上也无法和\"全\n新一套\"形成对照。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— fond 看似\"心爱的衣服\"很顺，实则破坏对照。fond 形容词\n没错、与 clothing 搭也勉强能读通\"心爱的衣服\"，但本句的关键是和下半句 a whole new\nwardrobe 形成\"现有 vs 全新\"的对照——只有 current 能撑起这条对照。防坑：填完看一\n眼上下句逻辑关系，是不是该有的对照丢了。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词修饰名词 clothing"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) wardrobe\n名词\n【词性槽】限定词 a whole new 后必接名词\n【依据】一整套衣物=wardrobe\n上句说能不能\"通过 current clothing 改善健康\"，下句紧跟 without having to dash out\nand buy a whole new __(28)__——意思是\"不必跑去买一整套新的\"，与\"clothing\"对仗的\n整体性名词正是 wardrobe（个人全部衣物的集合）。\n【竞争词】A) accessories 复数与单数框架冲突\naccessories 是复数名词，无法接在 a whole new __(28)__ 这种\"a + 单数\"框架后；且\"配饰\"只\n是衣着的一部分，撑不起\"全新\"这个量级。\nH) locations 话题不沾边\nlocations\"地点\"与\"买衣服改善健康\"的话题毫无语义关联，且同样是复数，与 a + 单数的框架\n不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·限定词 a whole new 后必接名"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accessories\n名词\n【词性槽】并列名词的最后一项\n【依据】配饰=与 bows/ties 同类\nbows（蝴蝶结）、ties（领带）本来就是典型的服装 accessories（配饰），并列\"and\nunnecessary accessories\"= \"以及多余的配饰\"，语义、词类完全一致。\n【竞争词】H) locations 语义场完全不沾\nlocations\"地点\"与\"穿着上的小装饰\"完全不是一类；并列结构里第三项必须与前两项同类，\nlocations 进不去这个语义场。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列名词的最后一项"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) perceive\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 you 后缺谓语动词原形\n【依据】perceive sth as sth=把……视为……\nperceive A as B 是地道搭配，意为\"把 A 看作/视为 B\"。opt for clothes you perceive as\ntying in with your goals\"选你认为与目标相契合的衣服\"——与后文\"想在工作中表现更好\n就挑你视为 professional 的衣服\"的论述无缝衔接。\n【竞争词】B) align 搭配错，且 align 多为不及物\nalign 常以 align with（与……一致）出现，而非 align sth as sth；句中已经有 as，不可能再嵌\n一个 align as 的搭配。\nN) showcase 语义\"展示\"与\"视为\"不同\nshowcase 是\"展示、陈列\"，showcase clothes as 表面上能成句，但意思变成\"把衣服展示\n为……\"，与上下文\"你**认为**这些衣服契合目标\"的内向认知动作不符。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— align 看着\"契合 goals\"很顺，但接不上 as。align 在 26 题\n已用，此处即便没用过也是陷阱——它的搭配是 align with，而原文 you __(30)__ as tying\nin with your goals 给的是 as，必须找 V…as… 型动词。防坑：盯住后面的介词/连词，让搭\n配说话，不要靠模糊语义\"读着顺\"下笔。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 you 后缺谓语动词原形"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) engaging\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 by/whereby 后需要动名词\n【依据】engage in a behavior=投身某种行为\nengage in 是地道固定搭配，意为\"从事、投身于\"，engaging in a behavior (in this case,\nselecting clothes) can set you on the path to then achieving your goals\"参与某一行为\n（这里指挑衣服）可以让你迈上实现目标的道路\"——与 behavioral activation\"行为激\n活\"的概念完美对应。\n【竞争词】L) prospering 搭配不成立、语义偏\"繁荣\"\nprosper in 通常表\"在某领域兴旺\"（如 prosper in business），不与 a behavior 这种抽象动作\n搭配；且语义\"在某行为里繁荣\"也与原文\"投身行为\"不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 by/whereby 后需要动名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) frame\n名词\n【词性槽】your + ___ + of mind\n【依据】frame of mind=心境/心态\nframe of mind 是地道固定搭配，意为\"心境、心情状态\"，improve your frame of\nmind\"改善心情\"与首段提出的 well-being 主题、和下句 shift your mood 完美呼应。\n【竞争词】K) profile 无 profile of mind 搭配\nprofile 常用 a high/low profile、a profile of sb，不与 of mind 构成习语；语义上\"心智侧\n写\"与\"靠换衣服改善\"也不对路。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·your + ___ + of mind"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) positively\n副词\n【词性槽】情态动词与实义动词之间填副词\n【依据】正向地改变心情\ncan positively shift your mood\"能积极地/朝好的方向改变你的心情\"——与全段\"换换衣\n服可以让你跳出 rut、心情向好\"的论调一致；positively shift 是地道修饰，强调改变的方向\n为正向。\n【竞争词】C) concurrently \"同时地\"与心情转变无关\nconcurrently 表\"同时\"，但本句并无两个动作并行的语义，shift your mood 是单一动作；放进\n去整句逻辑断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词与实义动词之间填副词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) reluctant\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词后 + to do\n【依据】\"不情愿出门面对世界\"\nbe reluctant to do = \"不情愿做某事\"，是地道固定搭配；On days when you're really\nreluctant to brave the world\"在你真的不情愿勇敢面对外界的日子里\"——正与后文\"穿能\n勾起美好回忆的衣服来汲取力量\"形成因果，逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】F) fond 搭配错、感情色彩反\nfond 一般用 be fond of + n.，不接 to do；况且 fond 是正面色彩，与下文需要\"借助情感联结\n的衣服来汲取力量\"暗示的低落情绪相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词后 + to do"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accessories", "B) align", "C) concurrently", "D) current", "E) engaging", "F) fond", "G) frame", "H) locations", "I) perceive", "J) positively", "K) profile", "L) prospering", "M) reluctant", "N) showcase", "O) wardrobe"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) fond\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词 associations\n【依据】fond associations=温馨回忆\nfond 作\"深情的、温馨的\"，与 associations/memories 是高频搭配，意为\"美好的、令人\n怀念的联想\"——和前句的 sentimental items of clothing（带情感意义的衣物）、tap\ninto constructive emotions（调动积极情绪）一脉相承。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词修饰名词 associations"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg's latest research suggests many psychologists and neuroscientists wrongly believe in the causal relationship between music and IQ.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·latest research + wrongly believe causal\n【定位】Recently, he decided to formally investigate just how often his fellow researchers in\npsychology and neuroscience make what he believes are erroneous—or at least premature—\ncausal connections between music and intelligence. His results, published in May, suggest\nthat many of his peers do just that.\n最近，他决定正式调查一下他在心理学和神经科学领域的同行们，多频繁地做出他认为是错\n误的——或者至少是过早的——关于音乐与智力之间的因果关联。他 5 月发表的结果表\n明，他的许多同行的确如此。\n【改写】Glenn Schellenberg's latest research ↔ Recently, he decided to formally investigate\n时间副词 + 行为动词改写\nwrongly believe in the causal relationship between music and IQ ↔\nerroneous—or at least premature—causal connections between music and intelligence\n同义词替换 + 范围保留\nmany psychologists and neuroscientists ↔\nhis fellow researchers in psychology and neuroscience\n同义短语改写\n【辨邻】 G G 段 the majority of the articles erroneously claimed that music training had a\ncausal effect 虽然也讲「错称因果」，但题干 36 强调的是「Schellenberg 的最新研\n究表明 + 同行错信」这一整体判断，落点在 E 段的 His results, published in May,\nsuggest that many of his peers do just that 上。\nthe majority of the articles erroneously claimed that music training had a causal effect", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The belief in the positive effects of music training appeals to many researchers who are musicians themselves.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·researchers who are musicians themselves\n【定位】Music researchers tend, like Schellenberg, to be musicians themselves, and as he noted in his\nrecent paper, \"the idea of positive cognitive and neural side effects from music training (and\nother pleasurable activities) is inherently appealing.\"\n音乐研究者们和 Schellenberg 一样，往往自己就是音乐家；正如他在最近的论文里所写：\n「音乐训练（以及其他令人愉悦的活动）带来正向认知与神经副作用的想法，本身就很有吸\n引力。」\n【改写】The belief in the positive effects of music training ↔\nthe idea of positive cognitive and neural side effects from music training\n近义词替换 + 概括\nappeals to many researchers who are musicians themselves ↔\nMusic researchers tend, like Schellenberg, to be musicians themselves is inherently appealing\n主被动转换 + 同源词替换\n【辨邻】 D D 段 as a passionate musician, Schellenberg was delighted when he turned up\ncredible evidence 是描述他作为音乐家时的个人情绪反应，而非题干的『许多本身是\n音乐家的研究者被这种正向看法所吸引』。\nas a passionate musician, Schellenberg was delighted when he turned up credible evidence", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg was doubtful about the claim that music education helps enhance children's intelligence.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·doubtful + claim that music education helps enhance children's intelligence\n【定位】Schellenberg had long been skeptical of the science supporting claims that music education\nenhances children's abstract reasoning, math, or language skills.\nSchellenberg 长久以来一直对那些声称音乐教育能提升儿童抽象推理、数学或语言能力的\n说法所依赖的科学持怀疑态度。\n【改写】was doubtful about the claim ↔ had long been skeptical of the science supporting claims\n近义形容词替换\nmusic education helps enhance children's intelligence ↔\nmusic education enhances children's abstract reasoning, math, or language skills\n上位词总称 ↔ 下位词枚举\n【辨邻】 I I 段 Schellenberg remains highly critical of how the concept of plasticity has\nbeen applied in his field 的怀疑对象是脑可塑性的应用方式，而题干 38 的怀疑对象\n是「音乐教育能否提升儿童智力」这一更宽泛的命题，范围不同。\nSchellenberg remains highly critical of how the concept of plasticity has been applied in his field", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg came to the conclusion that most of the papers assessed made the wrong claim regarding music's effect on intelligence.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·most of the papers + wrong claim\n【定位】After computing their assessments, Schellenberg concluded that the majority of the articles\nerroneously claimed that music training had a causal effect.\n在汇总助手们的评估之后，Schellenberg 得出结论：大多数论文错误地宣称音乐训练具有\n因果效应。\n【改写】most of the papers assessed ↔ the majority of the articles\n近义词替换\nmade the wrong claim regarding music's effect on intelligence ↔\nerroneously claimed that music training had a causal effect\n名词短语 ↔ 副词 + 动词 + 因果改写\n【辨邻】 E E 段 many of his peers do just that 是『许多同行』的程度词，而题干 39 用的是\n『大多数论文（most of the papers）』这种定量化判决，应当对到 G 段更精确的\nmajority 表述。\nHis results, published in May, suggest that many of his peers do just that", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "You must abandon your unverified beliefs before you become a scientist.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·abandon unverified beliefs + become a scientist\n【定位】But those convictions should be checked at the entrance to the lab, he added. Otherwise,\nthe work becomes religion or faith. \"You have to let go of your faith if you want to be\na scientist.\"\n但他补充说，那些信念应该在走进实验室时就被搁置；否则，研究就会沦为宗教或信仰。\n「如果你想成为一名科学家，就必须放下你的信念。」\n【改写】You must abandon your unverified beliefs ↔ You have to let go of your faith\n情态动词 + 动词短语同义替换\nbefore you become a scientist ↔ if you want to be a scientist\n条件状语句式转换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Lots of experiments have demonstrated that people with music training can better differentiate certain sounds.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·music training + differentiate certain sounds\n【定位】And many experiments have shown that musical training improves certain hearing\ncapabilities, like filtering voices from background noise or distinguishing the difference\nbetween the consonants(辅⾳) 'b' and 'g'.\n并且许多实验已经表明，音乐训练能提升某些听觉能力，比如从背景噪声中过滤人声，或区\n分辅音 b 和 g 之间的差异。\n【改写】Lots of experiments have demonstrated ↔ many experiments have shown\n数量词 + 动词同义替换\npeople with music training can better differentiate certain sounds ↔\nmusical training improves certain hearing capabilities, like ... distinguishing the difference\nbetween the consonants(辅⾳) 'b' and 'g'\n上位概括 + 同义动作 + 例证还原\n【辨邻】 K K 段 improvements in hearing ability, as well as in more general competencies,\nsuch as the ability to concentrate 是一项追踪研究的整体发现，并未提到『区分某\n些声音』这种具体辨别动作；这正是 H 段独有的细节。\nimprovements in hearing ability, as well as in more general competencies, such as the ability\nto concentrate", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg's findings at the beginning of this century were not supported by a larger study carried out some ten years later.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·beginning of this century + larger study ten years later\n【定位】But nearly a decade later, in 2013, the Education Endowment Foundation funded a bigger\nstudy with more than 900 students. That study failed to confirm Schellenberg's findings,\nproducing no evidence that music lessons improved math and literacy skills.\n但将近十年后，在 2013 年，Education Endowment Foundation 资助了一项更大规模的\n研究，受试者超过 900 名学生。那项研究未能证实 Schellenberg 的发现，没能拿出证据表\n明音乐课提升了数学和读写能力。\n【改写】Glenn Schellenberg's findings at the beginning of this century ↔\nThe 2004 paper was specifically designed to address those concerns\n时间状语的上位概括\nwere not supported by a larger study carried out some ten years later ↔\nnearly a decade later, in 2013, the Education Endowment Foundation funded a bigger study\nwith more than 900 students\n时间表达 + 形容词近义替换\nwere not supported by a larger study ↔ That study failed to confirm Schellenberg's findings\n否定结构同义替换\n【辨邻】 B B 段 The IQ of the children in the two musical groups rose by an average of\nseven points 描述的是 2004 实验的正面结果，并未提及十年后大研究是否支持；题\n干 42 的关键事件是『未被支持』，应落在 D 段。\nThe IQ of the children in the two musical groups rose by an average of seven points in the course of\na year", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "One researcher shares Glenn Schellenberg's view that it is necessary to conduct long-term developmental studies to understand the effects of music training.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·One researcher shares + long-term developmental studies\n【定位】Neuropsychologist Lutz Jancke agrees. \"Most of these studies don't allow for causal\ninferences,\" he said. For over two decades, Jancke has researched the effects of music\nlessons, and like Schellenberg, he believes that the only way to truly understand their effects\nis to run longitudinal studies.\n神经心理学家 Lutz Jancke 表示同意。「这些研究大多无法支持因果推断，」他说。二十多\n年来，Jancke 一直在研究音乐课的效果，并且和 Schellenberg 一样，他认为真正理解其效\n果的唯一办法是做纵向研究。\n【改写】One researcher shares Glenn Schellenberg's view ↔ Neuropsychologist Lutz Jancke agrees\n动词同义替换 + 模糊化指代\nit is necessary to conduct long-term developmental studies to understand the effects of music\ntraining\n↔ the only way to truly understand their effects is to run longitudinal studies\n专业术语 ↔ 通俗描述\n【辨邻】 K K 段 Some researchers are starting to do just that 是承接 J 段后举出『正在做的\n人』，而题干 43 的关键是『有研究者赞同他 + 主张这种方法必要』，落点在 J 段\nJancke 的表态。\nSome researchers are starting to do just that", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg's research assistants had no idea what he was trying to prove in his new study.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·research assistants + no idea what he was trying to prove\n【定位】Schellenberg didn't tell his assistants what exactly he was trying to prove.\nSchellenberg 没有告诉他的助手们他究竟想证明什么。\n【改写】Glenn Schellenberg's research assistants had no idea ↔ Schellenberg didn't tell his assistants\n因果改写\nwhat he was trying to prove in his new study ↔ what exactly he was trying to prove\n宾语从句直接对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Glenn Schellenberg admits that practice can change certain areas of the brain but doubts that the change can affect other areas.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】强锚·practice can change certain areas + doubts other areas\n【定位】Practice does change the brain, he allows, but what is questionable is the assertion that\nthese changes affect other brain regions, such as those responsible for spatial reasoning or\nmath problems.\n他承认，练习确实能改变大脑；但他认为可疑的是那种断言——这些改变会影响到其他脑\n区，比如负责空间推理或数学问题的脑区。\n【改写】admits that practice can change certain areas of the brain ↔\nPractice does change the brain, he allows\n让步动词同义替换\ndoubts that the change can affect other areas ↔\nwhat is questionable is the assertion that these changes affect other brain regions, such as\nthose responsible for spatial reasoning or math problems\n动词 ↔ 名词化改写\n【辨邻】 H H 段 scientists frequently point to brain plasticity—the fact that the brain\nchanges according to how we use it 描述的是科学家如何为因果辩护，并未出现\n『但是 + 怀疑跨区影响』这种让步—转折；题干 45 的双层结构只在 I 段完整呈现。\nscientists frequently point to brain plasticity—the fact that the brain changes according to how we\nuse it\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 开篇引子段：点出全文将要呈现的总观点——一项近期分析发现，大多数关于音乐与\n能力提升的研究在因果关系上存在表述偏差。\n信息指纹 总纲句·研究表述偏差\nB 回顾 2004 年那篇标题为「音乐课提升智商」的论文：Schellenberg 把 144 个孩子随\n机分进键盘、声乐、戏剧、对照四组，发现两个音乐组的智商平均涨了 7 分，另外两\n组涨 4.3 分。\n信息指纹 具象·2004 实验与四组分配\nC 交代 Schellenberg 长期以来对「音乐教育能提升孩子抽象推理、数学、语言能力」这\n一说法持怀疑态度，强调相关不等于因果。\n信息指纹 态度·长期怀疑\nD 2004 年那篇论文本是为了回应这些质疑，并一度让他作为音乐爱好者欣慰；但 2013\n年由 Education Endowment Foundation 资助的、900 多名学生的更大规模研究并未\n支持他的结论。\n信息指纹 反转·2013 大样本未复现\nE\nSchellenberg 平静接受这个消息，转而正式调查同行心理学家与神经科学家有多频繁\n地做出错误或仓促的「音乐—智商」因果判断；他 5 月发表的研究结果显示，确实有\n许多同行如此。\n信息指纹 新研究·调查同行误判频率\nF 介绍新研究的具体做法：让两位助手收集自 2000 年以来 114 篇相关论文，看摘要标\n题里有没有「enhance/promote/facilitate/strengthen」这种暗示因果的动词，并按是\n否用脑成像归入神经科学或心理学；他没告诉助手自己想证明什么。\n信息指纹 方法·114 篇 + 关键动词 + 助手盲评\nG\n公布新研究的核心结论：大多数论文都错误地宣称音乐训练具有因果效应；神经科学\n论文里这种「过度推销」尤其严重，四分之三把单纯的相关说成因果；并附带指出心\n理学和神经科学过去受到的批评程度不同。\n信息指纹 结论·多数论文错称因果，神经科学更严重\nH 讲科学家如何论证因果：常借助「大脑可塑性」概念，举例小提琴训练会让左手手指\n运动相关脑区生长，多项实验显示音乐训练能改善某些听觉能力，如从背景噪声中过\n滤人声、区分辅音 b 和 g。\n信息指纹 机制·脑可塑性与听觉辨别例证\nI Schellenberg 对脑可塑性在该领域的应用提出严厉批评：练习确实能改变大脑，但\n「这些改变会影响其他脑区（如空间推理、数学）」这一断言才是可疑之处。\n信息指纹 反驳·承认局部改变但质疑跨区迁移\nJ 神经心理学家 Lutz Jancke 同意 Schellenberg 的看法：现有研究多数无法支持因果推\n断，要真正理解音乐课的效果，必须做纵向（longitudinal）研究——长期追踪有无音\n乐课的孩子群体，再比较结果。\n信息指纹 盟友·Jancke 主张做纵向研究\nK 举一项正在做的纵向研究：海德堡大学 Peter Schneider 跟踪一群孩子十年，部分孩子\n参与德国鲁尔区的「每个孩子一件乐器」项目；其中那些真心热爱并自愿练习的孩\n子，在听觉能力和注意力等更普遍能力上都有提升。\n信息指纹 实例·Schneider 十年追踪 + 鲁尔区项目\nL\n另一项正在做的纵向研究：南加州大学 Assal Habibi 用洛杉矶低收入社区的孩子，分\n音乐组、体育组、无课后项目组三组对照，两年后报告音乐组孩子大脑出现结构性\n变化。\n信息指纹 实例·Habibi 三组对照纵向研究\nM 对 Habibi 研究泼冷水：她的孩子不是随机选的，那些被音乐吸引的孩子或许本就有某\n种与众不同的特质；Schellenberg 用自己 5 岁起每天 7 点起床练琴的经历举例，质疑\n这种经历是否能在所有人身上系统地产生同样的改变。\n信息指纹 再质疑·非随机 + 个人经历类比\nN\n顺着上段反思：音乐研究者大多自己就是音乐家（Schellenberg 也是），他在新论文\n里写道「音乐训练（和其他愉悦活动）带来正向认知与神经副作用的想法本身就很有\n吸引力」；他也承认若自己有孩子也会鼓励他们学音乐和上大学。\n信息指纹 自省·研究者多为音乐家这一吸引力偏差\nO 结尾告诫：但那些信念应该在走进实验室时就被搁置，否则研究就变成宗教或信仰；\n想做科学家就必须放下自己的信仰。\n信息指纹 结论·进实验室前先放下信念", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What did Neil Postman say about the rise of television?", "options": ["A) It initiated a change from dominance of reason to supremacy of pleasure.", "B) It brought about a gradual shift from cinema going to home entertainment.", "C) It started a revolution in photographic technology.", "D) It marked a new age in the entertainment industry."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did Neil Postman say about the rise of television? 指向 Postman 关于电视\n兴起的具体观点，属典型的「找人找观点」细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What did Neil Postman say about the rise of television? 是一个特殊疑问句，主干\n为 What did Neil Postman say，about the rise of television 是介词短语作 say 的状语，\n限定言说的话题。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】As Neil Postman noted in his 1985 book Amusing Ourselves to Death, the rise of television\nintroduced not just a new medium but a new discourse: a gradual shift from a typographic(印\n刷的) culture to a photographic one, which in turn meant a shift from rationality to emotions,\nexposition to entertainment.\n正如尼尔·波兹曼在其 1985 年出版的《娱乐至死》一书中所指出的，电视的兴起带来的不仅\n是一种新媒介，更是一种新话语：一种从印刷文化到图像文化的渐变，而这又意味着一种从\n理性到情感、从论述到娱乐的转向。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na shift from rationality to emotions, exposition to entertainment ↔\na change from dominance of reason to supremacy of pleasure\n预判要求「从理性到娱乐/情感」，a change from dominance of reason to\nsupremacy of pleasure 同时锁住起点 reason 与终点 pleasure，是定位句的标准同义\n改写。\nB) 偷换概念\ncinema going to home entertainment 把话题从「理性 vs 娱乐」偷换成「看电影 vs\n居家娱乐」，原文从未讨论 cinema going。\nC) 偷换概念\na revolution in photographic technology 把原文 typographic(印刷的) culture to a\nphotographic one 中 photographic（图像化的，与 typographic 印刷化相对）误读\n为「摄影技术」。原文谈的是文化形态而非摄影术革命。\nD) 以偏概全\na new age in the entertainment industry 把原文对「娱乐文化全面侵入理性」的批判\n性论述，矮化为「娱乐产业新时代」这一中性的产业事件，既缩小了范围（仅产业），\n又抽走了「理性被取代」的核心立场。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "According to the passage, what is the advantage of text reading?", "options": ["A) It gives one access to huge amounts of information.", "B) It allows more information to be processed quickly.", "C) It is capable of enriching one's life.", "D) It is conducive to critical thinking."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 what is the advantage of text reading? 问的是原文已明示的具体功能，无\ninfer/suggest/imply 等推断信号，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 According to the passage, what is the advantage of text reading? 主干为 what is\nthe advantage，According to the passage 是状语，of text reading 限定 advantage。\n结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】It is text that enables us to uncover lies, confusions and overgeneralizations, and to detect\nabuses of logic and common sense. It also means to weigh ideas, to compare and contrast\nassertions, to connect one generalization to another.\n正是文本使我们能够识破谎言、混乱与过度概括，发现逻辑与常识被滥用的地方。它还意味\n着权衡观点、比较与对比断言、把一个概括与另一个概括连接起来。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\naccess to huge amounts of information 谈的是「获取大量信息的渠道」，原文 It is\ntext that enables us to 引出的功能清单从未提「信息量」这个维度，是无中生有。\nB) 无中生有\nmore information to be processed quickly 把功能定位到「处理速度」，原文从未对\n比「快慢」，也没说文本能让人更快地处理信息，属无中生有。\nC) 过度推断\ncapable of enriching one's life 把「识破谎言、权衡观点」这种认知层面的具体功能，\n泛化为「丰富人生」这种笼统好评，跨度过大；原文未谈生活质量。\nD) ✓ 正确\nto detect abuses of logic and common sense ↔ It is conducive to critical thinking\nto weigh ideas, to compare and contrast assertions ↔ critical thinking\n预判要求「批判性思考、识破谎言、权衡观点」，四个选项里只有 critical thinking 落\n在这条语义线上，其余三项都跑题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How has television impacted Americans?", "options": ["A) It has given them a lot more to argue about.", "B) It has brought celebrities closer to their lives.", "C) It has made them care more about what they say.", "D) It has rendered their interactions more superficial."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How has television impacted Americans? 用现在完成时问已经造成的影响，原文应\n有直接对应的描述，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 How has television impacted Americans? 为现在完成时疑问句，主干 How has\ntelevision impacted Americans。现在完成时强调「已经发生且仍有影响」，提示原文相\n应句也描述既成结果。\n【定位】Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other. They don't exchange ideas, they\nexchange images. They do not argue with propositions, they argue with good looks,\ncelebrities and commercials.\n美国人不再彼此交谈，而是彼此娱乐。他们不再交换思想，而是交换形象。他们不再用命题\n展开辩论，而是用漂亮的外表、名人和广告来「辩论」。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文明说 They do not argue with propositions——美国人不再用命题辩论；a lot\nmore to argue about 反而说「有更多可争辩的」，方向相反。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 they argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials 中 celebrities 只是\n「用以替代命题的几种表面元素」之一，并未谈「名人与生活更近」；brought\ncelebrities closer to their lives 是把单词从原句中抠出来另起一个意思。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 Americans don't talk to each other, we entertain each other 暗示美国人不再\n认真说话；care more about what they say 却说他们「更在意自己说什么」，方向\n反了。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthey exchange images ↔ rendered their interactions more superficial\nthey argue with good looks, celebrities and commercials ↔ interactions more superficial\n预判要求「互动退化为表层」，四个选项里只有 rendered their interactions more\nsuperficial 用 superficial 一词命中这一内核。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the passage say about the World Wide Web?", "options": ["A) It was developed primarily for universities worldwide.", "B) It was created to connect people in different countries.", "C) It was viewed as a means to quest for knowledge.", "D) It was designed as a discussion forum for university students."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about the World Wide Web? 直接问文章怎么说，无\n推断信号，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the passage say about the World Wide Web? 主干为 What does the\npassage say，about the World Wide Web 是状语限定话题。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】When it emerged towards the end of the 1980s as a purely text-based medium, it was seen as\na tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure.\n当 Web 在 1980 年代末作为一种纯文本媒介出现时，它被视为一种追求知识、而非追求快\n乐的工具。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 Universities around the world were among the first to connect to this new\nmedium 只说大学是最早接入者，并未说 Web 是「为大学开发」的；developed\nprimarily for universities worldwide 把「使用者」误读为「开发目的」。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未谈 Web 的设计目的是「连接不同国家的人」；connect people in different\ncountries 与原文最相近的句子是 Universities around the world were among the\nfirst to connect to this new medium，但这里 connect 的宾语是 medium，而非\npeople in different countries。\nC) ✓ 正确\nit was seen as a tool to pursue knowledge, not pleasure ↔\nIt was viewed as a means to quest for knowledge\n预判即「被视为追求知识的工具」；It was viewed as a means to quest for\nknowledge 与预判几乎逐词同义，毫无歧义。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 which hosted discussion groups, informative personal or group blogs,\nelectronic magazines, and academic mailing lists and forums 只说 Web 上承载了\n讨论组与论坛等，并未说它「为大学生论坛而设计」；designed as a discussion\nforum for university students 既限定了用户（仅大学生），又限定了用途（仅论\n坛），双重以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn about users of social media?", "options": ["A) They are bent on looking for an alternative space for escape.", "B) They are constantly seeking approval from their audience.", "C) They are forever engaged in hunting for new information.", "D) They are unable to focus their attention on tasks for long."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about users of social media? 问文章已写明的用户行为，无推断\n信号，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about users of social media? 主干为 What do we learn，\nabout users of social media 是状语限定话题。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Instead of a quest for knowledge, it engages us in an endless zest(热情) for instant approval\nfrom an audience, for which we are constantly but unconsciously performing.\n它不再让我们追求知识，而是让我们沉浸于无尽的、向观众即时求赞的热情中——为了这些\n观众，我们持续不断却又毫无察觉地表演。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nalternative space 一词在原文出现于 created an alternative space that threatened\ntelevision's grip on society，描述的是早期 Web 而非社交媒体用户；looking for an\nalternative space for escape 把这一标签错挂到 social media users 头上。\nB) ✓ 正确\nan endless zest(热情) for instant approval from an audience ↔\nconstantly seeking approval from their audience\nwe are constantly but unconsciously performing ↔ constantly seeking approval\n预判要求「持续求得观众认可」，四个选项里只有 constantly seeking approval from\ntheir audience 命中 approval + audience 这对原词级抓手，毫无悬念。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明说 It reduces our curiosity by showing us exactly what we already want and\nthink，社交网络削弱用户好奇心、只投喂他们已知的东西；forever engaged in\nhunting for new information 却说他们持续追逐新信息，正好相反。\nD) 无中生有\nunable to focus their attention on tasks for long 谈的是「注意力难以长时间集中」\n——原文虽提及社交网络 refocuses our attention on videos and images，但并未\n说用户的注意力变得短暂或无法集中于任务，这是中文常见的「刻板印象」被读进了\n选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does a recent study find about a growing number of workers?", "options": ["A) They attempt to make more contributions.", "B) They feel they deserve more than they get.", "C) They attach importance to job satisfaction.", "D) They try to diminish workplace dysfunction."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does a recent study find about a growing number of workers? 是典型的事\n实询问，问研究发现了什么。\n【拆句】What does a recent study find about a growing number of workers? 是 What 引导的\n特殊疑问句，主语是 a recent study，谓语 find，about 短语限定研究对象是 a growing\nnumber of workers，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】According to a recent study, a small but growing proportion of the workforce is affected to\nsome degree by a sense of entitlement. Work is less about what they can contribute but more\nabout what they can take.\n据最近的一项研究，劳动力人群中有一小部分但正在增长的比例不同程度地被一种 \"理所应\n当 \" 的心态所影响。对他们而言，工作不再是关于他们能贡献什么，而更多是关于他们能从\n中索取什么。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 Work is less about what they can contribute 明确说 \"少贡献 \"，They attempt\nto make more contributions. 反说成 \"试图做出更多贡献 \"，方向完全相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\na sense of entitlement ↔ They feel they deserve more than they get.\nWork is less about what they can contribute but more about what they can take ↔\nmore than they get\n预判 \"觉得自己应得更多 \" 与 They feel they deserve more than they get. 字面与语\n义双重吻合，锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 diminish their own job satisfaction 提到 \"降低他们自身的工作满意度 \"，是\nentitlement 心态的后果；They attach importance to job satisfaction. 偷换成 \"他们\n重视工作满意度 \"，把 \"满意度被削弱 \" 偷换为 \"重视满意度 \"。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 It can lead to workplace dysfunction 是说 entitlement 心态 \"导致 \" workplace\ndysfunction，They try to diminish workplace dysfunction. 反说成 \"他们试图减少\nworkplace dysfunction\"，方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why don't some employees work hard according to many scholars?", "options": ["A) They lack a strong sense of self-worth.", "B) They were spoiled when growing up.", "C) They have received unfair treatment.", "D) They are overindulged by their boss."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why don't some employees work hard according to many scholars? 限定观点来\n源，问的是原因事实，不是推断。\n【拆句】Why don't some employees work hard according to many scholars? 是 Why 引导的\n疑问句，含助动词否定 don't，主语 some employees，状语 according to many\nscholars 标明观点来源；结构清晰。\n【定位】It's a tendency which many scholars believe begins in childhood due to parents who\noverindulge their kids. This thereby leads them to expect the same kind of spoilt treatment\nthroughout their adult lives.\n这是一种许多学者认为始于童年的倾向，源于过度溺爱孩子的父母；这进而导致他们在整个\n成年生活里都期待同样被宠爱的待遇。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文并未把 entitled 员工的偷懒归因于 \"缺乏自我价值感 \"；恰恰相反，inflated sense\nof self-worth 指出他们 \"自我价值感膨胀 \"，They lack a strong sense of self-worth.\n是反向编造。\nB) ✓ 正确\nparents who overindulge their kids ↔ They were spoiled when growing up.\nbegins in childhood ↔ when growing up\n预判的两个要素 \"童年 + 被父母溺爱 \"，分别对应 when growing up 与 were\nspoiled，They were spoiled when growing up. 全部落定。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文确有 legitimately dissatisfied with their employment conditions due to, say,\nbeing denied fair pay 提到 \"被不公正对待 \" 的另一类员工，但作者明确把这类人排除\n在讨论之外；They have received unfair treatment. 把另一群体的原因贴到本题群体\n身上。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说被溺爱者是 parents who overindulge their kids，是 parents 而非 boss；\nThey are overindulged by their boss. 把 \"父母溺爱 \" 偷换成 \"老板溺爱 \"，对象\n错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is a manager supposed to do to enable workers to do a better job?", "options": ["A) Be aware of their emotions.", "B) Give them timely promotions.", "C) Keep a record of their performance.", "D) Seek ways to sustain their motivation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is a manager supposed to do to enable workers to do a better job? 问管\n理者应该做什么，回原文找原文给出的方法陈述句即可。\n【拆句】What is a manager supposed to do to enable workers to do a better job? 主干是\nWhat is a manager supposed to do，be supposed to 表 \"应该\"；to enable workers\nto do a better job 是不定式作目的状语，使役结构 enable sb. to do。\n【定位】And yet despite how these employees feel, it's obviously important for their manager to\nnonetheless find out how to keep them motivated. And, by virtue of that heightened\nmotivation, to perform well.\n尽管这些员工有这样的感受，他们的管理者仍然必须想办法让他们保持工作动力；正是凭借\n这种被提高的工作动力，他们才能表现良好。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文并未要求管理者去 \"察觉员工情绪 \"；Be aware of their emotions. 中的 emotions\n与 \"觉察情绪 \" 在原文中均无对应，纯属编造。\nB) 无中生有\n原文没有提及 \"及时晋升 \" 这一手段；Give them timely promotions. 把 \"维持动力 \"\n具体化成了 \"及时给予晋升 \"，是无中生有兼过度推断。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未要求管理者 \"记录员工表现 \"；Keep a record of their performance. 把\nperform well（结果）误读成了 record of performance（手段），方向也错位。\nD) ✓ 正确\nfind out how to keep them motivated ↔ Seek ways to sustain their motivation.\nto perform well ↔ do a better job\n预判 \"想办法维持员工动力 \" 与 Seek ways to sustain their motivation. 在 \"寻方法 \"\n与 \"维持动力 \" 两个语义槽位双双对齐，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do the research findings reveal about ethical leaders?", "options": ["A) They are held accountable by their employees.", "B) They are always transparent in their likes and dislikes.", "C) They convey their requirements in a straightforward way.", "D) They make it a point to be on good terms with their employees."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do the research findings reveal about ethical leaders? 问研究结论中关于某\n对象的事实，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】What do the research findings reveal about ethical leaders? 是 What 引导的疑问句，\n谓语 reveal，about ethical leaders 是介词短语作宾语补足，限定 reveal 的对象。\n【定位】The findings revealed ethical leadership is precisely what alleviates the negative effects of\nemployee entitlement. That's because rather than indulging employees or neglecting them,\nethical leaders communicate very direct and clear expectations.\n研究发现，道德型领导力正是缓解员工 \"理所应当 \" 心态负面影响的解药。这是因为道德型\n领导既不纵容员工也不忽视他们，而是非常直接、清晰地传达期望。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 They also hold employees accountable for their behaviors 说 ethical leaders\n\"对员工的行为问责 \"——是 \"领导让员工担责 \"；They are held accountable by their\nemployees. 反说成 \"领导被员工问责 \"，主被动方向反转。\nB) 过度推断\n原文说 ethical leaders 的 expectations 直接清晰，并未说他们在 likes and dislikes\n（个人好恶）上始终透明；They are always transparent in their likes and dislikes. 把\n\"沟通期望直接 \" 扩张到 \"个人好恶始终透明 \"，超出原文范围。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncommunicate very direct and clear expectations ↔\nThey convey their requirements in a straightforward way.\n预判 \"直接清晰地传达要求 \" 与 They convey their requirements in a\nstraightforward way. 三个语义槽位（传达 / 要求 / 直接）全部对齐，锁定 C。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有提到 ethical leaders \"刻意与员工打好关系 \"。rather than indulging\nemployees 恰恰否定了 \"纵容 / 讨好 \" 这一路；They make it a point to be on good\nterms with their employees. 把 \"原则导向 \" 错换成了 \"关系导向 \"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What kind of leaders are viewed as ethical by entitled employees?", "options": ["A) Those who can be counted on to fulfill commitments.", "B) Those who can do things beyond normal expectations.", "C) Those who exercise caution in making major decisions.", "D) Those who know how to satisfy their employees' needs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What kind of leaders are viewed as ethical by entitled employees? 问哪一类领导\n被特定群体视为符合某属性，按原文中描述这种被视为关系的句子定位即可。\n【拆句】What kind of leaders are viewed as ethical by entitled employees? 主干 What kind of\nleaders are viewed as ethical，被动结构 are viewed as，by entitled employees 是动作\n发出者；结构需注意被动语态的方向：是 \"被 entitled employees 视为\"，不是反过来。\n【定位】This shift away from unrealistic expectations is successful because entitled employees feel\nmore confident that ethical leaders will deliver on their promises. This occurs because they're\nperceived to be fair and trustworthy.\n这种摆脱不切实际期望的转变之所以奏效，是因为 entitled 员工对 ethical leaders 能兑现\n承诺更有信心；之所以如此，是因为他们被视为公平且可信。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nethical leaders will deliver on their promises ↔\nThose who can be counted on to fulfill commitments.\n预判 \"兑现承诺、说到做到 \" 与 Those who can be counted on to fulfill\ncommitments. 在 \"被信赖 + 兑现承诺 \" 两个语义槽位双双对齐，锁定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 They'd then go further to explain the specific, and objective, criteria the\nemployee must meet to receive their desired rewards. 强调 ethical leaders 给的是\n\"客观且明确 \" 的标准；Those who can do things beyond normal expectations. 把\n\"按标准奖励 \" 错写成 \"超出常规预期 \"，方向相反。\nC) 张冠李戴\nexercise caution 是文末研究者本人的态度（提醒 no single response is the perfect\nremedy），不是 entitled 员工对 ethical leaders 的评价；Those who exercise\ncaution in making major decisions. 把研究者的特征贴到了 ethical leaders 身上。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 an ethical leader is significantly disinclined to accommodate their demands\n明确说 ethical leaders \"显著不愿迁就员工需求 \"；Those who know how to satisfy\ntheir employees' needs. 反说成 \"懂得满足员工需求 \"，方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
